ke chat histories.\n3. **Query Analysis & Migration:** MySQL `EXPLAIN` tests if indexes are working, while Sequelize `up`/`down` migrations manage database changes.\n4. **Dependencies vs. Dev Dependencies:** Production dependencies are required for the app to run; dev dependencies are helper tools used only during coding.\n5. **Hybrid AI Architecture:** MERN stack applications connect React and Node.js with Python AI microservices for machine learning tasks.','2026-10-12 11:31:46','2026-10-12 11:31:46',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(267,4,'user','more','2026-10-12 13:51:18','2026-10-12 13:51:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(268,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Machine Learning Classification, Deep Learning\n\nHere are more main points covering the remaining key topics:\n\n1. **LLM Core Concepts:** Hallucinations (false AI outputs) are reduced using RAG and fine-tuning, while guardrails act as safety filters for inputs and outputs.\n2. **Tokens & Context Windows:** LLMs process text in chunks called tokens, and managing context windows is essential for handling memory and API costs efficiently.\n3. **Machine Learning (SVC):** Used structured datasets (like the Titanic exercise) for data cleaning, feature engineering, and classification modeling.\n4. **Deep Learning (Toxic Comment Classification):** Utilized NLP techniques like text tokenization, padding, and deep learning architectures (ANN, CNN, LSTM) to classify text, evaluating performance with accuracy and confusion matrices.\n5. **Project Management & Backlog:** Tracked medium-priority backlog issues covering OpenRouter API integration, MongoDB conversation history, and token-based context management.','2026-10-12 13:51:19','2026-10-12 13:51:19',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(269,4,'user','more details','2026-10-12 13:52:49','2026-10-12 13:52:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(270,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Database Indexing, MERN Stack Integration, Deep Learning (Toxic Comment Classification)\n\nHere are more details regarding those main points:\n\n1. **Database Indexing Details:**\n   * **B-Trees:** The standard data structure for database indexes, keeping data sorted to allow quick lookups, insertions, and deletions in logarithmic time.\n   * **MySQL `EXPLAIN`:** A command used before a SQL query to see how the database engine executes it, helping developers verify if an index is actually being used or if a slow full-table scan is happening.\n   * **Sequelize Migrations (`up` / `down`):** Code files that track database changes over time. The `up` function applies changes (like creating an index), while the `down` function rolls them back if something goes wrong.\n\n2. **LLM & AI Integration Details:**\n   * **Chunking & Tokens:** LLMs read text broken down into small pieces called tokens (roughly chunks of characters or words). Because models have a maximum context window (memory limit), long documents must be split (chunked) to fit within these limits.\n   * **APIs & Guardrails:** LLM APIs let your app talk to a remote AI server. Guardrails sit in the middle to block toxic user inputs or strip out sensitive data before it reaches the AI.\n\n3. **MERN Stack & Python Hybrid Architecture Details:**\n   * **Frontend to Backend:** The React frontend sends user requests (like a prompt or comment to classify) to the Node.js/Express backend.\n   * **Python Microservice:** Since deep learning libraries (like TensorFlow or PyTorch) live in Python, Node.js forwards the request to a Python API (using FastAPI or Flask) which loads the trained model, runs inference, and returns the result.\n   * **Database Storage:** MongoDB is used to store application state, user history, chat logs, and classification results.\n\n4. **Deep Learning (Toxic Comment Classification) Details:**\n   * **Text Preprocessing:** Raw text strings must be cleaned, tokenized (turned into numbers), and padded (made into uniform lengths) so neural networks can process them.\n   * **Model Architectures:** Uses networks like Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) with LSTMs or Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to understand the sequential context of words in a sentence.','2026-10-12 13:52:51','2026-10-12 13:52:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(271,4,'user','check','2026-10-12 13:54:40','2026-10-12 13:54:40',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(272,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Database Indexing, MERN Stack Integration, Deep Learning (Toxic Comment Classification), Machine Learning Classification\n\nEverything looks accurate and aligns with the technical concepts, files, and project architectures we have discussed so far. \n\nIf you would like to check a specific piece of code, review another document, or dive deeper into any of these areas, just let me know what you\'d like to look at next!','2026-10-12 13:54:42','2026-10-12 13:54:42',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(273,4,'user','give me LLM content we discuss earlier','2026-10-12 13:55:58','2026-10-12 13:55:58',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(274,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Artificial Intelligence (AI)\n\nHere is a summary of the core LLM and AI content we discussed earlier:\n\n1. **What is an LLM?**\n   * Large Language Models are advanced AI systems trained on massive amounts of text data. They understand context, answer questions, write code, and generate human-like text.\n\n2. **Core LLM Concepts:**\n   * **Tokens:** The pieces of text (words or parts of words) that an LLM reads and generates. Models count tokens to measure input and output length.\n   * **Context Window:** The maximum amount of text (measured in tokens) an LLM can process and remember at one time during a single interaction.\n   * **Chunking:** The practice of breaking large documents into smaller pieces so they can fit inside an LLM\'s context window.\n   * **Hallucination:** When an AI model generates false, fabricated, or incorrect information and presents it as a factual truth. This is often reduced using retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and fine-tuning.\n   * **Guardrails:** Safety filters placed around an AI model to block harmful user inputs, filter out toxic AI responses, and protect private data.\n\n3. **LLM APIs:**\n   * Tools that allow developers to send text prompts from their application to a remote AI model (like OpenAI or OpenRouter) and receive generated responses back via code.','2026-10-12 13:56:00','2026-10-12 13:56:00',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(275,4,'user','now guardrails','2026-10-12 13:56:31','2026-10-12 13:56:31',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(276,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Guardrails, Artificial Intelligence (AI), Large Language Models (LLMs)\n\nHere is a detailed look at **Guardrails** based on our earlier discussions:\n\n### What are Guardrails?\n**Guardrails** are safety filters and rule-checks placed around an AI model (like an LLM) to control what goes into the model and what comes out. Think of them like guardrails on a highway—they keep the AI from driving off a cliff or wandering into dangerous territory.\n\n### Why Do We Need Them?\nLLMs are powerful, but they can be unpredictable. Without guardrails, an application using an AI model might:\n* Accept harmful or malicious prompts from users.\n* Generate toxic, biased, or inappropriate responses.\n* Accidentally leak sensitive information (like passwords, API keys, or private user data).\n* Make up false facts (hallucinations) and present them as dangerous medical or financial advice.\n\n### How Guardrails Work (The Two-Way Street)\n\n1. **Input Guardrails (Before the AI sees the prompt):**\n   * **Content Filtering:** Scans incoming user messages for hate speech, violence, prompt injection attacks (tricks trying to break the AI\'s rules), or inappropriate content.\n   * **Sanitization:** Strips out private data (like credit card numbers or social security numbers) before sending the text to the LLM API.\n\n2. **Output Guardrails (Before the user sees the response):**\n   * **Toxicity Check:** Scans the AI\'s generated response to ensure it isn\'t rude, harmful, or biased.\n   * **Fact-Checking / Grounding:** Checks if the AI\'s response matches the provided documents (useful in RAG systems) to reduce hallucinations.\n   * **Format Validation:** Ensures the AI outputs the exact format you need (such as strict JSON) so your application code doesn\'t crash.\n\nIn short, guardrails act as a secure wrapper around AI models, ensuring that applications built with LLMs remain safe, reliable, and user-friendly.','2026-10-12 13:56:34','2026-10-12 13:56:34',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(277,4,'user','Hallucination','2026-10-12 13:59:41','2026-10-12 13:59:41',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(278,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Hallucination, Artificial Intelligence (AI), Large Language Models (LLMs)\n\nHere is a detailed look at **Hallucination** based on our earlier discussions:\n\n### What is Hallucination?\nIn the context of Artificial Intelligence and Large Language Models (LLMs), a **hallucination** happens when the AI generates information that is completely false, fabricated, or incorrect, but presents it with absolute confidence as if it were factual truth. \n\nFor example, if you ask an LLM about a fictional event or obscure historical fact, instead of saying *\"I don\'t know,\"* it might smoothly invent realistic-sounding names, dates, and details that never actually happened.\n\n### Why Do LLMs Hallucinate?\nLLMs do not \"think\" or look up facts in a traditional database the way a search engine does. Instead, they are massive prediction engines. Their goal is simply to predict the **most statistically likely next word or token** based on the patterns they learned during training. \n\nIf a prompt pushes the model into unfamiliar territory, or if it tries to fill in a gap in its training data, it will keep generating text that *sounds* correct based on grammar and structure, even if the underlying facts are entirely made up.\n\n### The Dangers of Hallucination\n* **Misinformation:** Users might rely on fake facts for school, work, or research.\n* **Risky Advice:** In fields like healthcare or finance, a fabricated fact or incorrect recommendation can lead to serious real-world harm.\n* **Broken Trust:** Users lose confidence in an application if they catch the AI lying.\n\n### How to Reduce and Prevent Hallucinations\nDevelopers use several techniques to keep AI models grounded in reality:\n1. **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG):** Instead of relying only on the AI\'s internal memory, the application searches an official document or database first, pastes that factual text into the prompt, and tells the AI: *\"Answer the question using **only** the provided text.\"*\n2. **Prompt Engineering:** Giving the AI clear instructions like, *\"If you do not know the answer based on the text, state \'I don\'t know\' rather than guessing.\"*\n3. **Fine-Tuning:** Training the model further on verified, domain-specific data so it becomes an expert in that specific subject area.\n4. **Guardrails:** Using output filters to catch and block unverified claims or force the model to cross-check its facts.','2026-10-12 13:59:44','2026-10-12 13:59:44',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(279,4,'user','summarize option in llm','2026-10-12 14:10:29','2026-10-12 14:10:29',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(280,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Artificial Intelligence (AI), Document Analysis\n\nHere is a simple explanation of how **summarization** works in Large Language Models (LLMs):\n\n### What is LLM Summarization?\nSummarization is the process where you give a large piece of text—such as an article, a legal document, a meeting transcript, or an uploaded file—to an LLM, and ask it to condense the main points into a shorter, easy-to-read version while keeping the core meaning intact.\n\n### Why LLMs are Great at Summarizing\nUnlike older software that simply chopped off the last few paragraphs of a text, LLMs actually \"understand\" the language. They can:\n* Identify key themes and arguments.\n* Ignore fluff, filler words, and repetition.\n* Rewrite complex technical jargon into simple English.\n\n### Common Types of Summarization Prompts\nWhen working with LLMs, you can ask for different styles of summaries depending on your needs:\n1. **Bullet-Point Summary:** Great for quick reading and catching main points.\n2. **Executive Summary:** A short paragraph giving a high-level overview for business or project reviews.\n3. **Length-Restricted Summary:** Telling the model, *\"Summarize this document in exactly 3 sentences\"* or *\"Give me a 10-line summary.\"*\n4. **Role-Based Summary:** Telling the model, *\"Summarize this technical research paper as if you were explaining it to a 5-year-old\"* or *\"Summarize this from a developer\'s perspective.\"*\n\n### Challenges with LLM Summarization\n* **Context Window Limits:** If a document is longer than the model\'s **context window** (the maximum number of **tokens** it can process at once), the AI cannot read the whole thing in one go. \n* **The Solution (Chunking):** For very large documents, developers use **chunking**—breaking the document into smaller pieces, summarizing each piece individually, and then combining those summaries into a final overview.\n* **Hallucination Risk:** Sometimes, an LLM might accidentally invent a detail or misinterpret a sentence while summarizing, which is why having source documents or **guardrails** is important.','2026-10-12 14:10:31','2026-10-12 14:10:31',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(281,4,'user','what is the best prompt we give for the summarization','2026-10-12 14:11:43','2026-10-12 14:11:43',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(282,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Artificial Intelligence (AI)\n\nThere isn\'t just *one* single \"best\" prompt because it depends on what you want the summary to look like. However, the best prompts follow a specific structure to give you the most accurate, clear, and useful results.\n\nHere is a formula for the best summarization prompt, followed by a few copy-and-paste examples.\n\n---\n\n### The Formula for a Great Summarization Prompt\nA powerful prompt usually contains four parts:\n1. **Role:** Tell the AI who it is (e.g., *\"Act as an expert researcher\"*).\n2. **Task:** Clearly state what you want (e.g., *\"Summarize the attached document\"*).\n3. **Constraints:** Tell it what to avoid or include (e.g., *\"Use bullet points, keep it under 5 sentences, and do not invent facts\"*).\n4. **Context / Source Text:** Provide the text or tell it to use the uploaded file.\n\n---\n\n### Best Prompt Examples (Copy & Paste)\n\n#### 1. The All-Purpose Executive Summary (Best for general documents)\n> \"Act as a professional analyst. Summarize the provided text into a clear, concise executive summary. Highlight the 3 to 5 most important takeaways using bullet points. If any critical information is missing, state \'Not mentioned in the text.\'\"\n\n#### 2. The Simple English / Beginner Summary (Best for complex technical files)\n> \"Act as a helpful teacher. Summarize the attached document in simple English so that a beginner can easily understand it. Avoid heavy jargon, and explain any technical terms used.\"\n\n#### 3. The Strict-Fact Summary (Best for avoiding hallucinations)\n> \"Read the attached document carefully and provide a summary of the main points. **Rule:** You must base your summary *strictly* on the provided text. Do not assume, extrapolate, or bring in outside knowledge. If you are unsure, leave it out.\"\n\n#### 4. The Length-Restricted Summary (Best for quick reading)\n> \"Summarize the core message of the text below into exactly 3 sentences. Be direct and cut out all unnecessary filler words.\"\n\n---\n\n### Why these prompts work well:\n* They **remove ambiguity** so the AI doesn\'t guess what format you want.\n* They use **constraints** (like strict fact-checking) to prevent **hallucinations**.\n* They control the **length and style** (bullet points vs. paragraphs) so you don\'t get a wall of text you didn\'t ask for.','2026-10-12 14:11:46','2026-10-12 14:11:46',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(283,4,'user','what about this:\r\nYou are updating a conversation summary.\r\n\r\nPrevious summary:\r\n${conversation.summary || \"No previous summary.\"}\r\n\r\nNew messages:\r\n${conversationText}\r\n\r\nCreate an updated summary.\r\n\r\nKeep:\r\n- Important topics\r\n- Important questions\r\n- Important answers\r\n- User preferences\r\n- Decisions\r\n- Important facts\r\n- Ongoing tasks\r\n\r\nDo not invent information. keep the wording simple and summary short.\r\n\r\nReturn only the updated summary.','2026-10-12 14:12:49','2026-10-12 14:12:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(284,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Artificial Intelligence (AI)\n\nThat is an **excellent prompt**! In fact, it is very close to the exact type of prompt professional developers use to build memory and conversation-tracking systems for AI applications. \n\nHere is why this is a great prompt, broken down by your criteria:\n\n### Why This Prompt Works So Well:\n\n1. **It Uses Incremental Updating (Rolling Summary):** \n   Instead of forcing the AI to read through the entire chat history from the very beginning every single time (which wastes tokens and slows things down), it takes the **Previous summary** and combines it only with the **New messages**. This is efficient and scales well for long chats.\n2. **It Sets Clear Boundaries (The \"Keep\" List):** \n   By explicitly telling the AI to keep topics, questions, answers, user preferences, decisions, facts, and tasks, you prevent it from throwing away critical context. The AI knows *what* matters.\n3. **It Enforces Strict Anti-Hallucination Rules:** \n   The instruction *\"Do not invent information\"* is vital. When summarizing memory, AI can sometimes try to \"fill in the gaps\" with things that weren\'t said. This rule stops that.\n4. **It Controls Formatting and Tone:** \n   *\"Keep the wording simple and summary short\"* ensures you get a clean, readable text block rather than a massive, messy essay.\n5. **It Ensures Clean Programmatic Output:** \n   *\"Return only the updated summary\"* is a favorite trick among developers. It stops the AI from writing conversational filler like *\"Sure! Here is your updated summary:\"* which would break your code if you are trying to automatically save that text directly into a database like Supabase or MongoDB.\n\n### When to Use This Prompt:\nThis prompt is ideal for **backend background jobs**—for example, right after a user finishes a chat session or every 10 messages, your Node.js or Python backend can automatically fire this prompt to an LLM API to keep your database updated with a fresh, compact memory state.','2026-10-12 14:12:52','2026-10-12 14:12:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(285,4,'user','what about this: You are updating a conversation summary. Previous summary: ${conversation.summary || \"No previous summary.\"} New messages: ${conversationText} Create an updated summary. Keep: - Important topics - Important questions - Important answers - User preferences - Decisions - Important facts - Ongoing tasks Do not invent information. keep the wording simple and summary short. Return only the updated summary.','2026-10-12 14:23:34','2026-10-12 14:23:34',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(286,4,'assistant','* **Topic Names:** Large Language Models (LLMs), Artificial Intelligence (AI)\n\nYes, that is the exact same prompt you just shared in your previous message! \n\nAs I mentioned before, **it is a fantastic, well-structured prompt.** It has all the right ingredients for a production-ready AI memory updater:\n* A clear **role** (*\"You are updating a conversation summary\"*)\n* Clear **inputs** (*Previous summary* + *New messages*)\n* Specific **rules for what to keep** (topics, decisions, tasks, etc.)\n* Strict **guardrails** (*\"Do not invent information\"*)\n* Formatting constraints (*\"keep the wording simple and summary short\"*, and *\"Return only the updated summary\"*)\n\nAre you planning to implement this in a specific part of your code (like a Node.js/Express backend or a Python script using an LLM API), or are you testing how it behaves?','2026-10-12 14:23:36','2026-10-12 14:23:36',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(287,1,'user','What technologies are mentioned in my resume?','2026-10-14 11:27:53','2026-10-14 11:27:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(288,1,'assistant','Based on your resume chunks provided, here are the technologies mentioned:\n\n**Programming Languages:**\n* C, C++, C#, JavaScript, Python\n\n**Frontend Development:**\n* HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5, React.js, JavaScript\n\n**Backend Development:**\n* Node.js, Express.js, Python, Django, RESTful APIs\n\n**Databases & Storage:**\n* MongoDB, Mongoose, PostgreSQL, Supabase, Supabase Storage, MS SQL Server, SQLite\n\n**Authentication & Security:**\n* JWT (JSON Web Tokens), bcrypt, Cookie-Based Authentication, Supabase Auth, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), Row Level Security (RLS)\n\n**Real-Time & Communication Technologies:**\n* Socket.IO, Supabase Realtime, WebRTC\n\n**Tools & Platforms:**\n* Git, GitHub, VS Code, Postman','2026-10-14 11:27:56','2026-10-14 11:27:56',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(289,1,'user','and education?','2026-10-14 11:28:25','2026-10-14 11:28:25',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(290,1,'assistant','Based on the resume chunks provided, here is your education:\n\n* **Degree:** Bachelor of Information Engineering Technology\n* **Institution:** Superior