tains).\n* **JavaScript** is the behavior and functionality (the electricity, plumbing, and appliances that let you turn on lights, open doors, or run the dishwasher).\n\n### What does JavaScript do?\nWhenever you visit a website and something happens that doesn\'t require the page to completely reload, JavaScript is usually behind it. For example:\n* **Interactive elements:** Clicking a button to open a pop-up menu or toggling between \"Dark Mode\" and \"Light Mode.\"\n* **Animations:** Image sliders, fading effects, or moving graphics.\n* **Form validation:** Telling you instantly if you forgot to type your email address into a sign-up form or if your password is too short.\n* **Live updates:** Feeds that refresh automatically (like social media timelines or live sports scores) without you having to hit the refresh button.\n\n### Where does it run?\nOriginally, JavaScript only ran inside web browsers (like Chrome, Safari, or Firefox) on your computer or phone. Today, it can also run on servers (using a tool called **Node.js**), meaning developers can use JavaScript to build both the front-end (what the user sees) and the back-end (databases and server logic) of applications. \n\n### A Quick Example\nIn JavaScript, a simple instruction to show a pop-up message on a screen looks like this:\n\n```javascript\nalert(\"Hello, World!\");\n```\n\nIn short, JavaScript is one of the core technologies of the modern web, making websites engaging, responsive, and functional.','2026-11-05 08:48:14','2026-11-05 08:48:14',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(622,28,'user','Explain the second project in my resume','2026-11-05 08:48:41','2026-11-05 08:48:41',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(623,28,'user','some examples related to this','2026-11-05 08:50:35','2026-11-05 08:50:35',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(624,28,'user','some examples for javascript','2026-11-05 08:50:53','2026-11-05 08:50:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(625,28,'user','what is js','2026-11-05 08:51:09','2026-11-05 08:51:09',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(627,30,'user','what is js','2026-11-05 08:53:26','2026-11-05 08:53:26',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(628,30,'assistant','**JS** stands for **JavaScript**. \n\nIt is one of the most popular programming languages in the world, primarily used to make websites interactive and dynamic. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes JavaScript so important and how it works:\n\n### 1. What does it do on a website?\nIf web development were a house:\n* **HTML** is the **structure** (the walls, roof, and rooms).\n* **CSS** is the **style** (the paint, furniture, and interior design).\n* **JavaScript** is the **behavior** (the electricity, plumbing, and working appliances that make the house functional).\n\nWhen you use a website, JavaScript is usually responsible for things like:\n* **Pop-up menus** and image sliders.\n* **Form validation** (checking if you typed your email address correctly before submitting).\n* **Dynamic content updates** (like liking a post on social media without having to reload the whole page).\n* **Animations** and interactive games played right in your browser.\n\n### 2. Where else is it used?\nWhile JavaScript was originally designed only to run inside web browsers (like Chrome, Safari, or Firefox), it has grown much bigger:\n* **Node.js:** Allows developers to use JavaScript to build the **backend** (server-side) of websites and applications, not just the front-end.\n* **Mobile Apps:** Frameworks like React Native let developers build mobile apps for iOS and Android using JavaScript.\n* **Desktop Apps:** Popular apps like VS Code and Discord are built using JavaScript technologies.\n\n### Simple Example\nImagine clicking a button that says \"Click Me!\" to change a heading\'s text. In JavaScript, that looks something like this:\n\n```javascript\n// Find the button and listen for a click\ndocument.getElementById(\"myButton\").addEventListener(\"click\", function() {\n    // Change the text on the page\n    document.getElementById(\"myHeading\").innerText = \"Hello, World!\";\n});\n```\n\nIn short, JavaScript is the versatile language that brings static web pages to life and powers a huge portion of modern software development!','2026-11-05 08:53:31','2026-11-05 08:53:31',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(629,30,'user','examples','2026-11-05 08:53:42','2026-11-05 08:53:42',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(630,30,'user','examples','2026-11-05 08:57:36','2026-11-05 08:57:36',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(631,30,'user','examples','2026-11-05 08:58:42','2026-11-05 08:58:42',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(632,30,'user','examples','2026-11-05 08:59:06','2026-11-05 08:59:06',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(634,32,'user','all projects in resume','2026-11-05 09:00:47','2026-11-05 09:00:47',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(635,32,'assistant','Based on the resume, here are the projects listed:\n\n### 1. Facebook Clone — Full Stack Social Media Application\n* **Technologies:** React.js, Supabase, PostgreSQL, Supabase Auth, Supabase Realtime, Supabase Storage, RLS, Bootstrap 5\n* **Key Features:**\n  * Developed a full-stack social media platform featuring user authentication, profiles, posts, comments, friendships, messaging, and notifications.\n  * Implemented post management (privacy settings, likes, saves, shares, comments, replies, and mentions) and social interactions (friend requests, user blocking).\n  * Integrated Supabase Realtime for instant messaging and real-time notifications.\n  * Added user reporting and administrative moderation features (account suspension and restoration).\n  * Secured application data and access using PostgreSQL, Supabase Storage, Row Level Security (RLS), and protected routes.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Real-Time Chat Application — MERN Stack & WebRTC\n* **Technologies:** React.js, Node.js, Express.js, MongoDB, Mongoose, Socket.IO, WebRTC, JWT\n* **Key Features:**\n  * Built a full-stack real-time messaging application.\n  * Implemented real-time one-to-one messaging using Socket.IO, along with message read receipts and status updates.\n  * Integrated peer-to-peer audio calls using WebRTC (including call initiation, incoming call handling, acceptance, and termination).\n  * Secured application access with JWT-based authentication and protected routes.\n  * Developed backend APIs and integrated MongoDB for storing user and message data.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Task Management System — MERN Stack\n* **Technologies:** React.js, Node.js, Express.js, MongoDB, Mongoose, JWT, bcrypt, Multer\n* **Key Features:**\n  * Created a full-stack task and to-do management application with complete CRUD functionality.\n  * Implemented secure user authentication using JWT-based cookie authentication and bcrypt password hashing.\n  * Designed user-specific task/to-do features and administrative functionality with role-based access control (RBAC) and module-level permissions.\n  * Integrated Multer for profile image uploads.\n  * Built an interactive dashboard displaying task and to-do statistics (completed vs. pending items).\n\n---\n\n### 4. ResumEase — AI-Powered HR Assistant\n* **Technologies:** Python, Django, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5, JavaScript, SQLite, AI/ML\n* **Key Features:**\n  * Developed an AI-powered recruitment platform for resume screening and interview scheduling.\n  * Implemented resume uploading, candidate information extraction, and skill-matching based on job requirements.\n  * Shortlisted candidates based on education, skills, and professional experience.\n  * Designed responsive dashboards for recruiters and users.\n  * Built a database-driven application using Django and SQLite.','2026-11-05 09:01:03','2026-11-05 09:01:03',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(636,32,'user','summary of resume','2026-11-05 09:01:22','2026-11-05 09:01:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(637,32,'user','summary','2026-11-05 09:04:40','2026-11-05 09:04:40',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(638,32,'assistant','Based on the provided document, here is the professional summary for the Full Stack Web Developer:\n\n* **Profile:** A motivated and detail-oriented Full Stack Web Developer with hands-on experience in building responsive, user-focused web applications. Currently gaining professional experience as a MERN Stack Developer Intern at ArhamSoft.\n* **Core Skills:** Skilled in developing RESTful APIs, authentication systems, CRUD applications, role-based access control, real-time applications, and database-driven systems.\n* **Technologies & Tools:** Works with React.js, JavaScript, Node.js, Express.js, Python/Django, MongoDB, and Supabase.','2026-11-05 09:04:51','2026-11-05 09:04:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(639,32,'user','what is javascript','2026-11-05 09:04:56','2026-11-05 09:04:56',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(640,32,'assistant','**JavaScript** is a popular programming language primarily used to make websites interactive and dynamic. \n\nIf you think of a webpage like a house:\n* **HTML** is the basic structure (the walls and rooms).\n* **CSS** is the styling and design (the paint, furniture, and curtains).\n* **JavaScript** is the electricity and plumbing—it makes things happen when you interact with the house.\n\n### What is JavaScript used for?\n* **Interactive Elements:** Opening menus, displaying pop-up windows, and clicking buttons that change content without reloading the page.\n* **Web and Mobile Apps:** Powering full applications on both the frontend (what users see in the browser) and the backend (servers and databases, using technologies like Node.js).\n* **Animations:** Creating moving graphics, image sliders, and interactive maps.\n\nIn your resume (as seen in your background as a Full Stack and MERN Stack Developer), JavaScript is a core tool you use alongside frameworks and libraries like **React.js** (for frontend development) and **Node.js** and **Express.js** (for backend development).','2026-11-05 09:05:01','2026-11-05 09:05:01',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(641,32,'user','certificates in resume','2026-11-05 09:08:02','2026-11-05 09:08:02',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(642,32,'user','certificates in resume','2026-11-05 09:08:43','2026-11-05 09:08:43',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(643,32,'assistant','Based on your resume, here are the certifications listed:\n\n* **JavaScript Full Stack / Web Development** — NAVTTC\n* **AI Fundamentals: Foundations for Understanding AI** — Cisco / IBM SkillsBuild, 2026\n* **Advanced Certification in JavaScript & Full Stack Development (MEAN-MERN)** — Centre for Digital Literacy & Business Management (September 2025 – November 2025), Grade: A+','2026-11-05 09:08:53','2026-11-05 09:08:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(644,32,'user','give few example code for js','2026-11-05 09:09:10','2026-11-05 09:09:10',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(645,32,'assistant','Here are a few simple examples of JavaScript to show how it works:\n\n### 1. Variables and Output (`console.log`)\nVariables are containers used to store data. You can print data to the console (developer tools) to see results.\n```javascript\nlet userName = \"Alice\";\nlet userAge = 22;\n\nconsole.log(\"Hello, \" + userName + \"! You are \" + userAge + \" years old.\");\n```\n\n### 2. Functions\nA function is a reusable block of code designed to perform a specific task. This function adds two numbers together:\n```javascript\nfunction calculateSum(num1, num2) {\n    return num1 + num2;\n}\n\nlet result = calculateSum(5, 10);\nconsole.log(result); // This will output: 15\n```\n\n### 3. Conditional Statements (`if/else`)\nConditions let your code make decisions based on whether something is true or false.\n```javascript\nlet userScore = 85;\n\nif (userScore >= 50) {\n    console.log(\"Congratulations, you passed!\");\n} else {\n    console.log(\"Sorry, please try again.\");\n}\n```\n\n### 4. Working with Arrays (Lists)\nAn array lets you store multiple items in a single variable. You can loop through them to do something with each item:\n```javascript\nlet techStack = [\"React.js\", \"Node.js\", \"MongoDB\", \"Express.js\"];\n\n// Loop through each technology and print it\ntechStack.forEach(function(tech) {\n    console.log(\"I am learning: \" + tech);\n});\n```','2026-11-05 09:09:15','2026-11-05 09:09:15',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(646,32,'user','more examples','2026-11-05 09:09:53','2026-11-05 09:09:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(647,32,'assistant','Here are a few more helpful JavaScript examples:\n\n### 1. Objects\nAn object allows you to store related data and behaviors together using key-value pairs. Think of it like a real-world entity, such as a user profile or a project.\n```javascript\nlet developer = {\n    name: \"John Doe\",\n    role: \"MERN Stack Developer\",\n    isIntern: true,\n    skills: [\"JavaScript\", \"React\", \"Node.js\"],\n    greet: function() {\n        console.log(\"Hi, my name is \" + this.name + \" and I am a \" + this.role + \".\");\n    }\n};\n\n// Accessing object properties and methods\nconsole.log(developer.name); // Output: John Doe\ndeveloper.greet();           // Output: Hi, my name is John Doe and I am a MERN Stack Developer.\n```\n\n### 2. Arrow Functions\nArrow functions are a shorter, cleaner way to write functions in modern JavaScript (introduced in ES6). \n```javascript\n// Traditional function\nfunction multiply(a, b) {\n    return a * b;\n}\n\n// Arrow function equivalent\nconst multiplyArrow = (a, b) => a * b;\n\nconsole.log(multiplyArrow(4, 5)); // Output: 20\n```\n\n### 3. Array Methods (`map` and `filter`)\nModern JavaScript has powerful built-in methods to transform or filter data in arrays without using traditional `for` loops.\n```javascript\nlet numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n\n// .map() creates a new array by transforming every item (e.g., doubling each number)\nlet doubledNumbers = numbers.map(num => num * 2);\nconsole.log(doubledNumbers); // Output: [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]\n\n// .filter() creates a new array with only the items that pass a test (e.g., numbers greater than 2)\nlet filteredNumbers = numbers.filter(num => num > 2);\nconsole.log(filteredNumbers); // Output: [3, 4, 5]\n```\n\n### 4. Template Literals\nTemplate literals allow you to embed variables and expressions directly inside strings using backticks (`` ` ``) instead of using the `+` sign to concatenate them.\n```javascript\nlet itemName = \"React.js\";\nlet itemType = \"Frontend Library\";\n\n// Using template literals\nlet message = `Technology: ${itemName} is a popular ${itemType}.`;\nconsole.log(message); // Output: Technology: React.js is a popular Frontend Library.\n```','2026-11-05 09:09:58','2026-11-05 09:09:58',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(648,32,'user','Advance example','2026-11-05 09:10:24','2026-11-05 09:10:24',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(649,32,'assistant','Here are a few advanced JavaScript examples that demonstrate concepts commonly used in modern web development, such as **Asynchronous JavaScript**, **Promises**, and **Destructuring**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Asynchronous JavaScript (`async / await`)\nIn web development, tasks like fetching data from a server or database take time (asynchronous operations). `async` and `await` make it easy to write asynchronous code that looks and behaves like synchronous (step-by-step) code.\n\n```javascript\n// A mock function that simulates fetching user data from a server after 2 seconds\nfunction fetchUserData() {\n    return new Promise((resolve) => {\n        setTimeout(() => {\n            resolve({ id: 101, name: \"Alice\", role: \"Full Stack Developer\" });\n        }, 2000);\n    });\n}\n\n// An async function to handle the waiting process\nasync function displayUserInfo() {\n    console.log(\"Fetching user data...\");\n    \n    // \'await\' pauses the function until the promise is resolved\n    let user = await fetchUserData();\n    \n    console.log(`Success! Welcome back, ${user.name} (${user.role}).`);\n}\n\ndisplayUserInfo();\n// Output order:\n// 1. Fetching user data...\n// (Waits 2 seconds...)\n// 2. Success! Welcome back, Alice (Full Stack Developer).\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Destructuring (Objects and Arrays)\nDestructuring is a convenient way to extract values from arrays or properties from objects and assign them to distinct variables quickly. This is heavily used in frameworks like React.\n\n```javascript\n// Object Destructuring\nlet project = {\n    title: \"ResumEase\",\n    techStack: [\"React\", \"Node.js\", \"MongoDB\"],\n    status: \"Completed\"\n};\n\n// Extracting properties directly into variables\nlet { title, status } = project;\n\nconsole.log(title);  // Output: ResumEase\nconsole.log(status); // Output: Completed\n\n// Array Destructuring\nlet colors = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"];\nlet [firstColor, secondColor] = colors;\n\nconsole.log(firstColor);  // Output: red\nconsole.log(secondColor); // Output: green\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Closures\nA closure is a function that remembers and has access to the variables in its outer (enclosing) scope, even after that outer function has finished executing. It is often used for data privacy and creating stateful functions.\n\n```javascript\nfunction createCounter() {\n    let count = 0; // This variable is private to createCounter\n\n    return {\n        increment: function() {\n            count++;\n            console.log(`Current count: ${count}`);\n        },\n        decrement: function() {\n            count--;\n            console.log(`Current count: ${count}`);\n        }\n    };\n}\n\nlet myCounter = createCounter();\n\nmyCounter.increment(); // Output: Current count: 1\nmyCounter.increment(); // Output: Current count: 2\nmyCounter.decrement(); // Output: Current count: 1\n\n// Note: You cannot access the \'count\' variable directly from here (e.g., console.log(count) would fail), \n// keeping it safe and secure inside the closure.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Spread and Rest Operators (`...`)\nThe spread operator allows you to expand elements of an array or object, while the rest operator allows you to collect multiple elements into an array.\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Spread Operator (Combining arrays)\nlet frontendSkills = [\"HTML\", \"CSS\", \"JavaScript\"];\nlet fullStackSkills = [...frontendSkills, \"Node.js\", \"Express\", \"MongoDB\"];\n\nconsole.log(fullStackSkills); \n// Output: [\"HTML\", \"CSS\", \"JavaScript\", \"Node.js\", \"Express\", \"MongoDB\"]\n\n// 2. Rest Operator (Collecting remaining function arguments)\nfunction summarizeProject(projectName, ...teamMembers) {\n    console.log(`Project: ${projectName}`);\n    console.log(`Team Members: ${teamMembers.join(\", \")}`);\n}\n\nsummarizeProject(\"Task Management System\", \"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\");\n// Output:\n// Project: Task Management System\n// Team Members: Alice, Bob, Charlie\n```','2026-11-05 09:10:28','2026-11-05 09:10:28',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(650,32,'user','few more','2026-11-05 09:12:56','2026-11-05 09:12:56',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(651,33,'user','projects and certificates mention in resume','2026-11-05 09:13:21','2026-11-05 09:13:21',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(652,33,'assistant','Based on your resume, here are the projects and certifications listed:\n\n### **Projects**\n\n1. **Facebook Clone — Full Stack Social Media Application**\n   * **Technologies:** React.js, Supabase, PostgreSQL, Supabase Auth, Supabase Realtime, Supabase Storage, RLS, Bootstrap 5\n   * **Key Features:** Full-stack social platform (auth, profiles, posts, comments, friendships, messaging, notifications), post management with privacy settings, friend requests, real-time instant messaging/notifications via Supabase Realtime, administrative moderation, and data security using PostgreSQL, Supabase Storage, and Row Level Security (RLS).\n\n2. **Real-Time Chat Application**\n   * **Technologies:** MERN Stack & WebRTC *(Note: Detailed bullet points for this project were cut off in the snippet, but it involves real-time chat functionality).*\n\n3. **Task & To-Do Dashboard (Integrated with Multer)**\n   * **Key Features:** Integrated Multer for profile image uploads and built an interactive dashboard displaying task and to-do statistics (completed and pending items).\n\n4. **ResumEase — AI-Powered HR Assistant**\n   * **Technologies:** Python, Django, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5, JavaScript, SQLite, AI/ML\n   * **Key Features:** AI-powered recruitment platform for resume screening and interview scheduling, resume uploading/candidate information extraction, skill-matching and shortlisting based on education/skills/experience, responsive dashboards, and a database-driven Django/SQLite backend.\n\n---\n\n### **Certifications**\n\n* **JavaScript Full Stack / Web Development** — NAVTTC\n* **AI Fundamentals: Foundations for Understanding AI** — Cisco / IBM SkillsBuild, 2026\n* **Advanced Certification in JavaScript & Full Stack Development (MEAN-MERN)** — Centre for Digital Literacy & Business Management (September 2025 – November 2025) \n  * *Grade: A+*','2026-11-05 09:13:27','2026-11-05 09:13:27',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(653,33,'user','what is js and give somple example code','2026-11-05 09:13:39','2026-11-05 09:13:39',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(654,33,'assistant','### What is JavaScript (JS)?\n\n**JavaScript (JS)** is a programming language used primarily to make web pages interactive. \n\nThink of building a website like building a house:\n* **HTML** is the structure (the walls, floors, and doors).\n* **CSS** is the design and styling (the paint color, furniture, and curtains).\n* **JavaScript** is the electricity and appliances (the lights turning on, the automatic garage door, or the TV working when you press a button). \n\nWithout JavaScript, websites would just be static pages of text and pictures that you can look at. With JavaScript, websites can respond when you click buttons, submit forms, show animations, or update data without reloading the page.\n\n---\n\n### Simple Example Code\n\nHere is a very simple example of JavaScript in action. Imagine you have a button on a webpage, and you want something to happen when a user clicks it.\n\n#### 1. The HTML (The Button)\n```html\n<button id=\"myButton\">Click Me!</button>\n<p id=\"message\"></p>\n```\n\n#### 2. The JavaScript\n```javascript\n// 1. Find the button and the paragraph on the page using their IDs\nconst button = document.getElementById(\"myButton\");\nconst messageBox = document.getElementById(\"message\");\n\n// 2. Add an event listener to listen for a \"click\" on the button\nbutton.addEventListener(\"click\", function() {\n    // 3. Change the text inside the paragraph when clicked\n    messageBox.textContent = \"Hello! You successfully clicked the button!\";\n});\n```\n\n### What happens in this code?\n1. **`document.getElementById(...)`**: JavaScript looks at the webpage to find specific elements (the button and the text box).\n2. **`addEventListener(\"click\", ...)`**: JavaScript waits patiently until the user clicks that button.\n3. **`messageBox.textContent = ...`**: The moment the button is clicked, JavaScript updates the text on the screen instantly!','2026-11-05 09:13:43','2026-11-05 09:13:43',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(655,33,'user','more advance code examples','2026-11-05 09:13:56','2026-11-05 09:13:56',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(661,37,'user','create the summary for this: https://vercel.com/kb/guide/what-is-an-llm-tool','2026-11-05 10:48:50','2026-11-05 10:48:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(662,37,'assistant','Here is a summary of the Vercel guide **\"What is an LLM Tool?\"**:\n\n### **1. What is an LLM Tool?**\nAn LLM (Large Language Model) tool is a function registered with an AI model that connects it to real-world actions, external APIs, or databases. While base LLMs are limited to generating text and code, tools allow them to perform tasks like fetching live data, booking events, or updating databases.\n\n### **2. What is a \"Tool\" Specifically?**\nIn technical terms, a tool consists of:\n* A **named function** registered with the model.\n* A **schema** (such as JSON Schema or a function signature) describing what the function does and what parameters it requires.\n* **Execution capability**, meaning the model can decide when to call it based on a user’s prompt.\n\n### **3. How Tool Calling Works (The Step-by-Step Loop)**\n1. **Registration:** Developers define function names, descriptions, and input schemas.\n2. **User Prompt:** A user asks a question or gives an instruction.\n3. **Model Decision:** The model evaluates whether a tool is needed and outputs a structured request containing the tool name and required arguments.\n4. **App Execution:** The application runs the actual function using the arguments provided by the model.\n5. **Final Response Generation (Optional):** The output of the tool is sent back to the model so it can synthesize a final, natural-language answer for the user.\n\n### **4. Why Are Tools Useful?**\nTools transform passive text-generators into **intelligent agents** capable of reasoning, selecting the correct functions, passing proper arguments, and interacting directly with external systems. Common use cases include querying internal data, performing math calculations, searching documentation, and updating database states.','2026-11-05 10:48:55','2026-11-05 10:48:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(663,37,'user','complete summary for this link and must add important points:\r\nhttps://www.geeksforgeeks.org/data-analysis/advanced-eda/','2026-11-05 10:50:47','2026-11-05 10:50:47',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(685,48,'user','Tell me about Albert Einstein using Wikipedia.','2026-11-05 11:26:27','2026-11-05 11:26:27