on-making.\n*   **The Field:** It is an academic and research discipline that develops methods and software to enable machines to perceive their environment and act to achieve defined goals.\n*   **Goal:** A major long-term objective for many researchers is to achieve **Artificial General Intelligence (AGI)**—systems that can perform nearly any cognitive task as well as or better than a human.\n\n### Key Techniques\nAI research draws on computer science, mathematics, statistics, psychology, and neuroscience. Some of the most prominent techniques include:\n*   **Machine Learning:** Programs that improve their performance on tasks automatically through experience.\n*   **Neural Networks & Deep Learning:** Architectures inspired by the human brain that excel at pattern recognition, image classification, and processing complex data.\n*   **Large Language Models (LLMs):** Generative models (like those behind chatbots) trained on vast amounts of text to predict and generate human-like language.\n*   **Classical Methods:** Including symbolic AI, formal logic, probabilistic reasoning, and search/optimization algorithms.\n\n### Evolution of the Field\n*   **History:** AI was founded as an academic discipline in 1956. Its history has been marked by cycles of high optimism and funding followed by periods of disappointment and reduced support, known as \"AI winters.\"\n*   **Recent Growth:** Interest and capabilities accelerated significantly after 2012 due to advancements in hardware (GPUs), the success of deep learning, and later, the development of transformer architectures in 2017.\n\n### Applications and Impact\nAI is integrated into many modern technologies, including:\n*   Web search engines, recommendation systems, and virtual assistants.\n*   Autonomous vehicles, gaming (e.g., chess, Go), and robotics.\n*   Healthcare (drug discovery, protein structure prediction).\n*   Generative media creation (text, images, audio).\n\n### Ethics and Challenges\nThe rapid growth of AI has prompted significant discussions regarding:\n*   **Risks:** Ethical concerns regarding privacy, copyright, algorithmic bias, misinformation, and the lack of transparency in how models make decisions.\n*   **Societal Impact:** Concerns about technological unemployment, existential risks, weaponized AI, and the need for regulation and safety frameworks.','2026-11-05 12:24:56','2026-11-05 12:24:56',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(716,57,'user','Explain the main concepts discussed in this article: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning \r\n\r\nCompare the information on these two pages: \r\n\r\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software) \r\n\r\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angular_(web_framework) \r\n\r\nRead this webpage and list the technologies mentioned in it. \r\n\r\nFind the main advantages and disadvantages discussed in this article. \r\n\r\nExplain this webpage in simple English with practical examples. \r\n\r\nFor questions 4–6, provide a webpage URL along with the prompt.','2026-11-05 12:25:11','2026-11-05 12:25:11',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(717,57,'assistant','### 1. Main Concepts in Machine Learning\nThe Wikipedia article defines **Machine Learning (ML)** as a field of AI focused on developing statistical algorithms that learn from data to make predictions or decisions without being explicitly programmed for every specific task.\n\n**Main Concepts:**\n*   **Learning Paradigms:**\n    *   **Supervised Learning:** The model is trained on labeled data (input-output pairs) to learn a mapping rule.\n    *   **Unsupervised Learning:** The model finds hidden structures or patterns in unlabeled data.\n    *   **Reinforcement Learning:** An agent learns by interacting with an environment, receiving rewards or penalties based on its actions.\n*   **Generalization:** A core goal, meaning the model\'s ability to perform accurately on new, unseen data rather than just memorizing training examples.\n*   **Performance Trade-offs:** The balance between **bias** (too simple, underfitting) and **variance** (too complex, overfitting).\n*   **Feature Learning:** Techniques that automatically discover the best ways to represent input data (like images or text) to make tasks easier.\n*   **Applications:** Includes data mining, anomaly detection (fraud detection), and pattern recognition.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Comparison: React vs. Angular\nBoth **React** and **Angular** are popular tools for building user interfaces, but they have fundamental differences in philosophy and structure.\n\n| Feature | React | Angular |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Type** | JavaScript Library (focused on UI). | Full-featured Web Application Framework. |\n| **Main Developer** | Originally Facebook (Meta), now the React Foundation. | Google. |\n| **Core Architecture** | Component-based, uses a Virtual DOM. | Component-based, uses TypeScript. |\n| **Data Binding** | Unidirectional (one-way) data flow. | Two-way data binding. |\n| **Language** | Primarily JavaScript/JSX. | TypeScript (strongly recommended). |\n| **Philosophy** | Flexible; requires third-party libraries for routing, etc. | Opinionated and \"batteries-included\" (includes routing, CLI, etc.). |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Technologies Mentioned in the React Wikipedia Page\n*URL: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software))*\n\nThe following technologies and related libraries/concepts are mentioned:\n*   **Languages:** JavaScript, JSX (JavaScript XML), PHP.\n*   **Core Concepts:** DOM (Document Object Model), Virtual DOM, Hooks, Server-Side Rendering (SSR), Server Components (RSC), Unidirectional data flow, Flux architecture.\n*   **Frameworks/Libraries:** Next.js, React Router, React Native, Redux, MobX, React Fiber (rendering engine), Jest, Flow, Immutable.js.\n*   **Infrastructure/Tools:** Git, GitHub, Linux Foundation, various browser APIs (event listeners, etc.).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Advantages and Disadvantages of React\n*URL: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software))*\n\n**Main Advantages:**\n*   **Performance:** Uses a Virtual DOM to efficiently update only the parts of the page that have changed, rather than re-rendering everything.\n*   **Component-Based:** Encourages creating modular, reusable pieces of code, making large applications easier to manage.\n*   **Flexibility:** Because it is a library rather than a full framework, developers can choose their own tools for routing, state management, and data fetching.\n*   **Ecosystem:** Has a massive community and ecosystem, providing many ready-made solutions.\n\n**Main Disadvantages:**\n*   **Complexity of Choice:** Because it isn\'t an \"all-in-one\" framework, beginners may struggle to decide which extra libraries to use (e.g., how to handle routing or state).\n*   **Learning Curve:** Concepts like JSX, Hooks, and the Virtual DOM can be confusing for those used to traditional HTML/JavaScript.\n*   **Constant Updates:** React changes frequently (e.g., the introduction of Hooks, Server Components, and new rendering algorithms), requiring developers to stay updated to follow bes','2026-11-05 12:25:34','2026-11-05 12:25:34',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(718,57,'user','Search Wikipedia for Artificial Intelligence. \r\n\r\nFind information about Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). \r\n\r\nSearch for Neural Networks and explain how they work. \r\n\r\nFind information about Exploratory Data Analysis. \r\n\r\nSearch for Natural Language Processing. \r\n\r\nFind information about Large Language Models.','2026-11-05 12:25:54','2026-11-05 12:25:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(719,57,'user','What are the main topics discussed in the uploaded document?','2026-11-05 12:26:55','2026-11-05 12:26:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(720,57,'user','What are the main topics discussed in the resume?','2026-11-05 12:27:40','2026-11-05 12:27:40',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(721,57,'assistant','Here is the information you requested based on your queries:\n\n### 1. Artificial Intelligence (AI)\n**Artificial Intelligence** refers to the capability of computer systems to perform tasks that typically require human intelligence. This includes capabilities like learning from experience, reasoning, solving problems, understanding natural language, and recognizing patterns. It is a broad field encompassing many sub-disciplines, including machine learning and natural language processing.\n\n### 2. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\n**Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)** is a technique used to improve the accuracy and relevance of Large Language Models (LLMs). Instead of relying solely on the information the model was originally trained on, RAG allows the model to \"retrieve\" up-to-date or specific information from an external, trusted database or document collection before generating an answer. This helps reduce errors (like hallucinations) and allows the model to provide responses based on current or private data.\n\n### 3. Neural Networks\n*Note: Due to a technical error, I could not access the specific Wikipedia page for this query, but I can explain the concept.*\n\n**Neural Networks** are a type of machine learning model inspired by the structure of the human brain. They consist of layers of interconnected \"nodes\" (or neurons).\n*   **How they work:** Data is fed into the **input layer**. It passes through one or more **hidden layers**, where the network performs mathematical calculations (adjusting weights and biases) to detect patterns. Finally, the **output layer** provides the prediction or result. Through a process called \"training,\" the network compares its output to the correct answer and adjusts its internal weights to improve its accuracy over time.\n\n### 4. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** is an approach used by data analysts to investigate and summarize the main characteristics of a data set. Instead of jumping straight to complex modeling, the goal of EDA is to get a \"feel\" for the data, often using visual methods (like charts and graphs) and summary statistics to identify patterns, spot anomalies (outliers), and test assumptions.\n\n### 5. Natural Language Processing (NLP)\n**Natural Language Processing (NLP)** is a subfield of AI focused on enabling computers to understand, interpret, generate, and manipulate human language. It bridges the gap between human communication and computer understanding, powering technologies like speech recognition, translation services, chatbots, and sentiment analysis.\n\n### 6. Large Language Models (LLMs)\n**Large Language Models (LLMs)** are a specific type of AI model—usually built using neural networks—trained on massive amounts of text data. Because they have \"read\" so much information, they can understand context, generate human-like text, answer questions, and perform various language tasks.\n\n---\n\n### Document Analysis\n\n**Main Topics in the Uploaded Document:**\n*(Note: As the search tool returned no results, I cannot identify an \"uploaded document\" other than the resume. If there is a different file you would like me to analyze, please let me know!)*\n\n**Main Topics in the Resume:**\nBased on the provided resume content, the main topics are:\n*   **Project Development:** Specifically the \"ResumEase\" project, an AI-powered HR assistant.\n*   **Technical Skills:** Proficiency in Python, Django, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5, JavaScript, SQLite, and AI/ML concepts.\n*   **Application Features:** Development of features such as resume screening, skill-matching, candidate shortlisting, interactive dashboards, and database management.\n*   **Education:** Bachelor of Information Engineering Technology from Superior University, Lahore, Pakistan (2021–2025).','2026-11-05 12:27:50','2026-11-05 12:27:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(722,57,'user','Summarize all the projects mentioned in my resume. \r\n\r\nWhat technologies are used in my projects? \r\n\r\nFind all information related to machine learning. \r\n\r\nWhat skills are mentioned in the document? \r\n\r\nCompare two projects mentioned in my resume. \r\n\r\nWhat information about education is available in the document? \r\n\r\nList all the important dates mentioned in the document.','2026-11-05 12:28:59','2026-11-05 12:28:59',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(723,57,'assistant','Based on your resume, here is the information you requested:\n\n### Projects Summary\nYour resume highlights four main projects:\n\n1.  **Facebook Clone:** A full-stack social media platform featuring user authentication, post management (privacy, likes, shares, comments), social interactions (friendships, blocking), real-time messaging/notifications, and administrative moderation tools.\n2.  **Real-Time Chat Application:** A full-stack MERN application enabling one-to-one messaging with read receipts and status updates, featuring peer-to-peer audio calls using WebRTC and JWT-based authentication.\n3.  **Task Management System:** A full-stack CRUD application with secure user authentication (JWT/bcrypt), role-based access control (RBAC), profile image uploads, and an interactive dashboard for task statistics.\n4.  **ResumEase (AI-Powered HR Assistant):** A recruitment platform designed for resume screening, interview scheduling, candidate information extraction, skill-matching, and shortlisting based on experience and education.\n\n### Technologies Used\n*   **Frontend:** React.js, HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript, Bootstrap 5.\n*   **Backend:** Node.js, Express.js, Python, Django.\n*   **Databases:** MongoDB, Mongoose, PostgreSQL, Supabase, SQLite, MS SQL Server.\n*   **Real-Time & Communication:** Socket.IO, Supabase Realtime, WebRTC.\n*   **Authentication & Security:** JWT, bcrypt, Cookie-Based Authentication, Supabase Auth, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), Row Level Security (RLS).\n*   **Tools: