e-collected. It supports multiple programming paradigms, including structured (particularly procedural), object-oriented, and functional programming. It is often described as a \"batteries included\" language due to its comprehensive standard library.\n\n### Python-Related Skills & Usage in Your Resume\nYour resume highlights Python as a core competency and demonstrates its application in your project work:\n\n*   **Skill Categories:**\n    *   **Backend Development:** You explicitly list **Python** and **Django** under your backend development skills.\n    *   **Programming Languages:** Python is listed among your core programming languages (alongside C, C++, C#, and JavaScript).\n*   **Project Usage:**\n    *   **ResumEase — AI-Powered HR Assistant:** This project is built using **Python** and **Django**, specifically leveraging them for AI/ML tasks such as resume screening, candidate information extraction, and skill-matching.','2026-11-05 12:30:32','2026-11-05 12:30:32',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(730,57,'user','Find information about neural networks on Wikipedia and identify related concepts in my resume documents.','2026-11-05 12:30:54','2026-11-05 12:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(731,57,'assistant','### Information about Neural Networks (Wikipedia)\n\nAccording to Wikipedia, a **Neural Network (NN)**, or **Artificial Neural Network (ANN)**, is a computational model inspired by the structure and functions of biological neural networks. These models are a foundational technology in **Machine Learning** and **Artificial Intelligence (AI)**, used to approximate complex, nonlinear functions.\n\nKey points include:\n*   **Purpose:** They are designed to perform various tasks by learning from data.\n*   **Structure:** They consist of artificial neurons, which are mathematical functions that serve as the elementary building blocks of the network.\n*   **Types:** Common architectures include Feedforward Neural Networks (FNN), Multilayer Perceptrons (MLP), and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN), which are often categorized under the umbrella of **Deep Learning**.\n\n---\n\n### Related Concepts in Your Resume\n\nBased on an analysis of your uploaded documents, here are the concepts related to neural networks and artificial intelligence that appear in your background:\n\n*   **Project - ResumEase (AI-Powered HR Assistant):**\n    *   This project explicitly lists **\"AI/ML\"** as a core technology used.\n    *   The features implemented—such as **resume screening**, **candidate information extraction**, and **skill-matching**—are classic applications of Machine Learning and AI models, which frequently utilize neural network architectures (like Natural Language Processing models for text extraction and classification).\n\n*   **Education & Certifications:**\n    *   You have completed a certification in **\"AI Fundamentals: Foundations for Understanding AI\"** (Cisco / IBM SkillsBuild, 2026). This course would cover the core principles of AI, machine learning, and the conceptual frameworks behind neural networks.','2026-11-05 12:31:12','2026-11-05 12:31:12',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(732,56,'user','Explain the main concepts discussed in this article: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning Compare the information on these two pages: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angular_(web_framework) Read this webpage and list the technologies mentioned in it. Find the main advantages and disadvantages discussed in this article. Explain this webpage in simple English with practical examples. For questions 4–6, provide a webpage URL along with the prompt.','2026-11-05 14:09:50','2026-11-05 14:09:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(733,58,'user','Explain the main concepts discussed in this article: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning Compare the information on these two pages: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angular_(web_framework) Read this webpage and list the technologies mentioned in it. Find the main advantages and disadvantages discussed in this article. Explain this webpage in simple English with practical examples. For questions 4–6, provide a webpage URL along with the prompt.','2026-11-05 14:12:01','2026-11-05 14:12:01',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(734,58,'user','Explain the main concepts discussed in this article: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning Compare the information on these two pages: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angular_(web_framework) Read this webpage and list the technologies mentioned in it. Find the main advantages and disadvantages discussed in this article. Explain this webpage in simple English with practical examples. For questions 4–6, provide a webpage URL along with the prompt.','2026-11-05 14:13:38','2026-11-05 14:13:38',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(735,58,'user','Explain the main concepts discussed in this article: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning Compare the information on these two pages: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angular_(web_framework) Read this webpage and list the technologies mentioned in it. Find the main advantages and disadvantages discussed in this article. Explain this webpage in simple English with practical examples. For questions 4–6, provide a webpage URL along with the prompt.','2026-11-05 14:17:15','2026-11-05 14:17:15',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(736,58,'assistant','### 1. Main Concepts in Machine Learning\nThe article on Machine Learning (ML) defines it as a field of artificial intelligence focused on developing statistical algorithms that learn from data to perform tasks without being explicitly programmed. Key concepts include:\n\n*   **Learning Paradigms:**\n    *   **Supervised Learning:** The model is trained on labeled data (input-output pairs) to learn a general rule for mapping inputs to outputs.\n    *   **Unsupervised Learning:** The model analyzes unlabeled data to discover hidden patterns or structures (e.g., clustering).\n    *   **Reinforcement Learning:** An agent learns to make decisions in an environment by interacting with it and receiving feedback in the form of rewards to maximize cumulative gain.\n*   **Core Concepts:**\n    *   **Generalization:** The ability of a model to perform accurately on new, unseen data based on the patterns it learned during training.\n    *   **Feature Learning:** Algorithms that automatically discover or transform raw data into useful representations for processing.\n    *   **Overfitting vs. Underfitting:** A balance where the model is complex enough to capture the data structure but not so complex that it merely memorizes the training noise.\n*   **Relationship to Other Fields:** ML is closely tied to statistics (which draws inferences) and data mining (which seeks previously unknown knowledge), though their goals differ.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Comparison: React vs. Angular\nReact and Angular are both popular tools for building web user interfaces, but they differ significantly in architecture and scope.\n\n| Feature | React | Angular |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Type** | JavaScript Library | Full-featured Web Framework |\n| **Developer** | Meta (and community) | Google (and community) |\n| **Language** | Primarily JavaScript/JSX | TypeScript (recommended) |\n| **Architecture** | Component-based; unopinionated | Component-based; highly structured |\n| **Data Binding** | Unidirectional (downwards) | Two-way data binding |\n| **Core Philosophy** | Library focused on UI; relies on third-party tools for things like routing | \"Batteries-included\"; includes routing, forms, and HTTP client out of the box |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Missing Information\nYou have requested information for \"Read this webpage and list the technologies mentioned in it,\" \"Find the main advantages and disadvantages,\" and \"Explain this webpage in simple English,\" but **you did not provide the URL for these specific tasks.**\n\nPlease provide the URL(s) you would like me to analyze for these three points, and I will be happy to assist you.','2026-11-05 14:17:19','2026-11-05 14:17:19',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(737,58,'user','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning\r\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software)','2026-11-05 14:18:04','2026-11-05 14:18:04',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(738,58,'assistant','Based on the Wikipedia articles provided, here is the explanation and comparison you requested.\n\n### 1. Main Concepts in Machine Learning\nMachine learning (ML) is a subfield of artificial intelligence focused on developing algorithms that can learn from data and improve their performance on tasks without being explicitly programmed for each step.\n\n*   **Learning Paradigms:**\n    *   **Supervised Learning:** Algorithms are trained on labeled data, where the desired output is known, to learn a rule that maps inputs to those outputs.\n    *   **Unsupervised Learning:** Algorithms analyze unlabeled data to discover hidden patterns, structures, or groupings without guidance.\n    *   **Reinforcement Learning:** An agent learns to make decisions by interacting with an environment to maximize cumulative rewards through trial and error.\n*   **Key Theoretical Concepts:**\n    *   **Generalization:** The ability of a trained model to perform accurately on new, unseen data.\n    *   **Feature Learning:** Algorithms that automatically discover or transform raw data into a useful representation for processing.\n    *   **Overfitting vs. Underfitting:** A critical challenge in balancing model complexity. A model must be complex enough to capture underlying patterns, but not so complex that it merely \"memorizes\" noise in the training data.\n*   **Relationship to Other Fields:** ML is closely tied to **statistics** (which draws inferences from samples) and **data mining** (which focuses on discovering unknown patterns), though they differ in their goals—ML often emphasizes predictive performance, while data mining often emphasizes exploratory knowledge discovery.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Comparison: React (Software)\nReact is a free, open-source JavaScript library primarily used for building user interfaces.\n\n*   **Core Philosophy:** It is specifically focused on the \"View\" layer of applications. It is **component-based**, meaning developers break down complex UIs into small, reusable pieces.\n*   **Key Features:**\n    *   **Declarative Programming:** Developers design views for each state of an application, and React automatically updates and renders the correct components when data changes.\n    *   **Virtual DOM:** React maintains an in-memory data structure (the Virtual DOM). When a component\'s state changes, React compares the new UI representation with the previous one and efficiently updates only the parts of the actual browser DOM that have changed, boosting performance.\n    *   **Unidirectional Data Flow:** Data flows in one direction (downward) through the component tree, making the code easier to debug compared to systems with bidirectional (two-way) data binding.\n    *   **JSX:** React uses JSX (JavaScript XML), a syntax extension that looks like HTML but allows developers to write the UI structure directly within JavaScript.\n*   **Flexibility:** Because React is a library rather than a full-featured framework, it does not include built-in solutions for routing or data management. Developers often rely on third-party libraries (like React Router) to add these capabilities, allowing them to choose the tools that fit their specific needs.','2026-11-05 14:18:08','2026-11-05 14:18:08',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(739,59,'user','Research MCP and create a report','2026-11-10 08:08:06','2026-11-10 08:08:06',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(740,59,'user','Research MCP and create a report','2026-11-10 08:10:05','2026-11-10 08:10:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(741,59,'assistant','# Report: Model Context Protocol (MCP)\n\n## Executive Summary\nThe **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** is an open standard and open-source framework introduced by Anthropic in November 2024. Its primary purpose is to standardize how AI models and assistants connect to external data sources, tools, and systems. By providing a universal protocol, MCP eliminates the need for developers to build and maintain custom integrations for every individual AI model or platform.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Background and Purpose\nHistorically, integrating AI models with external tools (such as databases, internal company repositories, or third-party APIs) required bespoke, \"point-to-point\" integrations. This created a fragmented ecosystem where developers had to rebuild connectors whenever they switched AI providers or added new capabilities.\n\nMCP was designed to solve this by creating a **standardized communication layer** between AI applications (clients) and data/tool providers (servers).\n\n## 2. Key Concepts\nMCP operates on a client-server architecture:\n\n*   **MCP Clients:** These are AI applications or platforms (e.g., Claude, IDEs like Cursor/VS Code, or custom AI agents) that want to access external data or tools.\n*   **MCP Servers:** These are lightweight programs that expose specific data or tools to the client. An MCP server might connect to a database (e.g., PostgreSQL), a file system, or a third-party service (e.g., GitHub, Slack, or Figma).\n*   **Standardized Communication:** Instead of proprietary APIs, MCP allows the client to \"ask\" the server for its capabilities, data, or tool executions using a common language.\n\n## 3. Core Objectives\n*   **Interoperability:** Enabling AI agents to move across different platforms without needing to re-configure tool access.\n*   **Reduced Complexity:** Simplifying the development cycle by allowing developers to build an integration once (as an MCP server) and use it with any MCP-compliant client.\n*   **Contextual Awareness:** By making it easier to connect AI to live data sources, MCP allows models to have better, real-time context for tasks, improving the quality and relevance of AI-generated responses.\n\n## 4. Industry Impact\nSince its introduction in late 2024, MCP has gained significant attention within the AI engineering community:\n\n*   **Tool Integration:** It is increasingly being used to bridge the gap between AI agents and enterprise data silos.\n*   **Standardization:** Major players and frameworks (such as LiteLLM) have begun adopting MCP support, indicating a move toward industry-wide adoption.\n*   **Ecosystem Growth:** Organizations are creating MCP servers to make their own systems discoverable and usable by AI agents, effectively creating a \"plug-and-play\" infrastructure for AI-powered automation.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThe Model Context Protocol represents a significant step toward a more modular and robust AI ecosystem. By abstracting the complexities of tool and data integration, MCP allows developers to focus on building advanced AI agents rather than maintaining fragile, custom infrastructure. As adoption grows, it is likely to become a foundational component of modern AI agent architectures.','2026-11-10 08:10:12','2026-11-10 08:10:12',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(742,59,'user','can you provide me the source link from which you give the above answer?','2026-11-10 08:18:34','2026-11-10 08:18:34',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(743,59,'assistant','The information provided in the report is based on the official documentation and the launch announcement for the Model Context Protocol (MCP). You can find the primary source material at the following links:\n\n*   **Official MCP Documentation:** [https://modelcontextprotocol.io/](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/)\n*   **Anthropic\'s Announcement:** [https://www.anthropic.com/news/introducing-the-model-context-protocol](https://www.anthropic.com/news/introducing-the-model-context-protocol)','2026-11-10 08:18:52','2026-11-10 08:18:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(744,59,'user','do you have a access for google search?','2026-11-10 08:19:20','2026-11-10 08:19:20',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(745,59,'assistant','I do not have a direct, general-purpose \"Google Search\" tool that I can use to browse the open web freely.\n\nHowever, I do have access to specialized tools that allow me to perform specific types of information gathering:\n\n1.  **Browse Tool:** I can use this to visit specific URLs and extract information from them, which is how I verified the links provided in our previous interaction.\n2.  **Wikipedia Search:** I can search Wikipedia directly for encyclopedic information.\n\nIf you have a specific topic you would like me to research, I can use these tools to find information from reliable sources or specific websites you point me toward!','2026-11-10 08:19:25','2026-11-10 08:19:25',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(746,59,'user','Which tools you have access','2026-11-10 08:23:54','2026-11-10 08:23:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(747,59,'assistant','I have access to the following tools to assist you:\n\n*   **Google Search (Browse):** I can use a search tool to find information across the web, visit specific websites, and extract content from them to answer your questions.\n*   **Wikipedia Search:** I can search Wikipedia directly to retrieve encyclopedic information on a wide range of topics.\n*   **Python Interpreter:** I can write and execute Python code. This is very useful for performing complex calculations, data analysis, generating charts, or processing information.\n*   **Document Search:** If you upload documents (like PDFs or text files) to our chat, I can analyze and search through those specific files to answer questions based on their content.\n\nIf you have a question or a task, feel free to let me know, and I can determine the best tool to help you!','2026-11-10 08:23:58','2026-11-10 08:23:58',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(748,60,'user','Which tools you have access','2026-11-10 08:30:06','2026-11-10 08:30:06',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(749,60,'assistant','I have access to the following tools:\n\n*   **Python Interpreter (`google:python_interpreter`):** Allows me to execute Python code to perform calculations, analyze data, or solve complex problems.\n*   **Web Browser (`google:browse`):** Enables me to access and extract information from websites.\n*   **Document Search (`default_api:searchDocuments`):** Allows me to search through uploaded documents to answer specific questions based on their content.\n*   **Wikipedia Search (`default_api:searchWikipedia`):** Provides the ability to search for and retrieve information from Wikipedia.','2026-11-10 08:30:08','2026-11-10 08:30:08',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(755,63,'user','Which tools you have access','2026-11-10 09:27:35','2026-11-10 09:27:35',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(756,63,'assistant','I have access to the following tools to assist you:\n\n*   **Google Search (Browse):** Allows me to search the internet and retrieve information from websites to provide up-to-date answers.\n*   **Google Search (Wikipedia):** Allows me to specifically search and retrieve information from Wikipedia.\n*   **Document Search:** Allows me to search through documents that you have uploaded to this conversation.\n*   **Python Interpreter:** Allows me to execute Python code to perform calculations, analyze data, or solve complex problems.','2026-11-10 09:27:42','2026-11-10 09:27:42',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'null',0),(757,64,'user','Explain Machine Learning.','2026-11-10 10:38:14','2026-11-10 10:38:14',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(758,64,'assistant','At its simplest, **Machine Learning (ML)** is a field of artificial intelligence (AI) that gives computers the ability to learn from data without being explicitly programmed for every specific task.\n\nInstead of writing step-by-step instructions (rules) for a computer to follow, you provide it with data and let it find patterns or rules on its own.\n\n---\n\n### How It Works: The Core Concept\n\nTo understand ML, it helps to compare it to traditional programming:\n\n*   **Traditional Programming:** You provide **Data + Rules** = **Output**.\n    *   *Example:* You write a program with an \"if-then\" rule: \"If the email contains the word \'win,\' mark it as spam.\"\n*   **Machine Learning:** You provide **Data + Output** = **Rules**.\n    *   *Example:* You feed a computer 10,000 emails already labeled as \"spam\" or \"not spam.\" The computer analyzes them, discovers that emails with words like \"prize,\" \"claim,\" and \"free\" are usually spam, and creates its own model to detect it in the future.\n\n---\n\n### The Three Main Types of Machine Learning\n\nMachine learning is generally categorized by how the \"learning\" process happens:\n\n#### 1. Supervised Learning (The \"Teacher\" Approach)\nIn this method, the model is trained on a **labeled dataset**. This means the data comes with the correct answers.\n*   **How it works:** The computer makes a prediction, compares it to the correct answer, and adjusts itself to reduce errors.\n*   **Example:** Predicting house prices based on historical sales data (size, location, number of bedrooms).\n\n#### 2. Unsupervised Learning (The \"Discovery\" Approach)\nHere, the model is given data **without any labels** or pre-defined answers.\n*   **How it works:** The computer looks for hidden patterns, groupings (clusters), or structures within the data on its own.\n*   **Example:** A retail store segmenting customers into groups based on purchasing habits (e.g., \"bargain hunters\" vs. \"luxury buyers\") without knowing those groups existed beforehand.\n\n#### 3. Reinforcement Learning (The \"Trial and Error\" Approach)\nThis is based on rewarding desired behaviors and punishing undesired ones.\n*   **How it works:** An \"agent\" interacts with an environment. If it performs a good action, it gets a \"reward.\" If it fails, it gets a \"penalty.\" Over time, it learns the best strategy to maximize rewards.\n*   **Example:** Teaching a robot to walk or training an AI to play video games.\n\n---\n\n### Real-World Examples\nYou interact with Machine Learning every day, often without realizing it:\n\n*   **Recommendation Engines:** Netflix or YouTube suggesting movies based on what you’ve watched before.\n*   **Personal Assistants:** Siri, Alexa, and Google Assistant using Natural Language Processing (a subset of ML) to understand your voice.\n*   **Healthcare:** AI models analyzing medical images (X-rays, MRIs) to detect diseases faster and more accurately than humans.\n*   **Finance:** Banks using ML to detect fraudulent credit card transactions in real-time.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nMachine Learning is not magic—it is **statistics applied at a massive scale**. By using powerful algorithms to process large amounts of data, machines can recognize patterns and make predictions that help us solve complex problems, automate repetitive tasks, and uncover insights that would be impossible for humans to find on their own.','2026-11-10 10:38:17','2026-11-10 10:38:17',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(759,65,'user','Explain Machine Learning.','2026-11-10 10:42:34','2026-11-10 10:42:34',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(760,65,'user','Explain Machine Learning.','2026-11-10 10:42:51','2026-11-10 10:42:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(761,65,'assistant','At its simplest, **Machine Learning (ML)** is a field of artificial intelligence that focuses on building systems that can learn from data, identify patterns, and make decisions with minimal human intervention.\n\nInstead of a human programmer writing strict, step-by-step rules for a computer to follow (like \"if this happens, do that\"), Machine Learning involves feeding the computer large amounts of data and letting it figure out the rules for itself.\n\nHere is a breakdown of how it works and the different ways it is used.\n\n---\n\n### How Machine Learning Works\nThink of it like teaching a child to recognize fruits:\n1.  **Traditional Programming:** You would write complex code describing every detail of an apple (round shape, red color, specific stem). If the computer saw a slightly different apple, it might fail.\n2.  **Machine Learning:** You show the computer thousands of pictures of apples and say, \"These are apples.\" You also show it pictures of oranges and say, \"These are not apples.\" The computer analyzes the pixels, identifies patterns common to the apples, and builds its own \"model\" of what an apple looks like.\n\n### Three Main Types of Machine Learning\n\n#### 1. Supervised Learning\nThe computer is trained on **labeled data**. This means you provide the input (e.g., an email) and the correct answer (e.g., \"spam\" or \"not spam\"). The computer learns the relationship between the inputs and the labels so that it can accurately predict the label for new, unseen data.\n*   **Example:** Predicting housing prices based on past sales data.\n\n#### 2. Unsupervised Learning\nThe computer is given **unlabeled data** and asked to find patterns or structures within it on its own. It isn\'t told what the \"right answer\" is; it simply organizes the information based on similarities.\n*   **Example:** A store grouping customers into segments (e.g., \"budget shoppers,\" \"frequent buyers\") based on their purchasing habits without being told what those segments are.\n\n#### 3. Reinforcement Learning\nThis is based on **trial and error**. The computer (often called an \"agent\") learns by interacting with an environment. It receives rewards for good actions and penalties for bad ones. Its goal is to maximize the total reward over time.\n*   **Example:** Training a computer to play a video game or teaching a robotic arm how to pick up objects.\n\n---\n\n### Real-World Examples\nYou encounter Machine Learning every day, often without realizing it:\n*   **Recommendation Engines:** Netflix or YouTube suggesting movies/videos based on what you have watched before.\n*   **Voice Assistants:** Siri or Alexa turning your spoken words into text and commands.\n*   **Fraud Detection:** Banks automatically flagging unusual transactions on your credit card.\n*   **Self-Driving Cars:** Systems that analyze road conditions, traffic lights, and obstacles to make driving decisions.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, Machine Learning is about moving from **\"programming\"** (telling the computer exactly what to do) to **\"training\"** (teaching the computer how to learn from experience).','2026-11-10 10:43:01','2026-11-10 10:43:01',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(762,65,'user','Explain Machine Learning using Wikipedia sources','2026-11-10 10:44:06','2026-11-10 10:44:06',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(763,65,'user','Explain Machine Learning using Wikipedia sources','2026-11-10 10:46:07','2026-11-10 10:46:07',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(764,66,'user','what is AI','2026-11-10 10:47:45','2026-11-10 10:47:45',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(765,66,'assistant','At its simplest, **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** is a branch of computer science that aims to create machines or software capable of performing tasks that typically require human intelligence.\n\nThink of it this way: traditional computer programs are like a rigid recipe—they follow exact, step-by-step instructions provided by a human to achieve a specific result. If something happens that isn\'t in the instructions, the program doesn\'t know how to handle it.\n\nAI, on the other hand, is designed to \"learn\" from data and make decisions or predictions based on patterns it identifies.\n\n### How Does It Work?\nMost modern AI is powered by something called **Machine Learning**. Instead of being explicitly programmed for every scenario, the system is fed massive amounts of data. It analyzes that data to find patterns and rules, which it then uses to improve its performance over time.\n\n### Common Examples You Likely Encounter\nYou are probably already using AI in your daily life:\n\n*   **Recommendations:** When streaming services like Netflix or Spotify suggest a new show or song you might like, they are using AI to analyze your past habits and compare them to millions of other users.\n*   **Virtual Assistants:** Tools like Siri, Alexa, or the AI you are talking to right now use Natural Language Processing (a subset of AI) to understand, interpret, and generate human language.\n*   **Navigation:** Apps like Google Maps use AI to analyze real-time traffic data, weather, and road conditions to find the fastest route for you.\n*   **Image Recognition:** AI can identify objects, faces, or even medical conditions (like spotting tumors in X-rays) by recognizing patterns in visual data.\n\n### Key Things to Remember\n*   **It isn\'t \"thinking\":** AI doesn\'t have consciousness, feelings, or true understanding. It is essentially doing extremely complex math and pattern recognition very quickly.\n*   **It depends on data:** Because AI learns from data, it can only be as good as the information it is given. If the data is biased or incorrect, the AI\'s conclusions will also be biased or incorrect.\n\nIn short, AI is a tool designed to process information, solve problems, and assist humans in ways that were previously only possible for biological brains.','2026-11-10 10:47:56','2026-11-10 10:47:56',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(766,66,'user','What is AI through wikipedia source','2026-11-10 10:49:13','2026-11-10 10:49:13',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(786,72,'user','what is ai','2026-11-10 13:14:59','2026-11-10 13:14:59',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(787,72,'assistant','Sorry, I could not generate a response.','2026-11-10 13:15:03','2026-11-10 13:15:03',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(788,72,'user','what is ai','2026-11-10 13:16:40','2026-11-10 13:16:40',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(789,72,'assistant','Sorry, I could not generate a response.','2026-11-10 13:16:50','2026-11-10 13:16:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(790,72,'user','what is ai','2026-11-10 13:17:49','2026-11-10 13:17:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(791,72,'assistant','**AI** stands for **Artificial Intelligence**. It refers to the simulation of human intelligence in machines—especially computer systems—designed to think, learn, reason, solve problems, and perform tasks that typically require human cognition.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what AI encompasses:\n\n### 1. How AI Works\nUnlike traditional computer programs that follow a strict, pre-written set of rules to get a specific output, AI systems (particularly modern ones) are trained using vast amounts of data. They look for patterns, make predictions, and improve their performance over time without being explicitly reprogrammed for every new scenario.\n\n### 2. Key Subfields of AI\n*   **Machine Learning (ML):** A subset of AI where computers use data and algorithms to \"learn\" how to do tasks automatically, improving with experience.\n*   **Deep Learning:** A more advanced type of machine learning inspired by the structure of the human brain (artificial neural networks). It powers complex tasks like image recognition, autonomous driving, and advanced language models.\n*   **Natural Language Processing (NLP):** The ability of computers to understand, interpret, and generate human language (e.g., translation tools, chatbots, and voice assistants like Siri or Alexa).\n*   **Computer Vision:** The capability of machines to \"see\" and interpret visual information from the world, such as videos and images.\n\n### 3. Types of AI\n*   **Narrow AI (Weak AI):** AI designed to perform a specific, narrow task (e.g., facial recognition, recommendation algorithms on Netflix, or playing chess). **All currently existing AI falls into this category.**\n*   **General AI (Strong AI):** A theoretical form of AI that would possess the ability to understand, learn, and apply knowledge across any intellectual domain just like a human being. It does not exist yet.\n\n### 4. Common Everyday Examples\n*   **Virtual Assistants:** Siri, Google Assistant, and Alexa.\n*   **Recommendation Systems:** Algorithms used by Netflix, YouTube, or Spotify to suggest movies, videos, or music based on your habits.\n*   **Generative AI:** Tools like ChatGPT, Claude, or Midjourney that can generate text, images, code, and music based on prompts.\n*   **Navigation:** Google Maps or Waze predicting traffic and finding the fastest routes.\n\nIn short, AI is a broad technological field aimed at creating machines capable of mimicking or augmenting human intelligence to make our lives easier, automate repetitive tasks, and solve complex global challenges.','2026-11-10 13:17:59','2026-11-10 13:17:59',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(792,72,'user','Explain Machine Learning using Wikipedia sources','2026-11-10 13:18:37','2026-11-10 13:18:37',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(793,73,'user','Explain Machine Learning using Wikipedia sources','2026-11-10 13:19:03','2026-11-10 13:19:03',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(794,73,'assistant','According to **Wikipedia**, **Machine Learning (ML)** is a subfield of artificial intelligence (AI) concerned with the development and study of statistical algorithms that can learn from data and generalize to unseen data, and thus perform tasks without explicit instructions. \n\nInstead of writing hard-coded rules and programs by hand, machine learning relies on algorithms building a mathematical model based on sample data (known as \"training data\") in order to make predictions or decisions.\n\n---\n\n### Key Learning Paradigms\nAccording to machine learning literature on Wikipedia, algorithms are traditionally categorized into three major paradigms based on the nature of the \"signal\" or \"feedback\" available to the learning system:\n\n1. **Supervised Learning:**\n   * **Definition:** The computer is presented with example inputs and their desired outputs (labels), and the goal is to learn a general rule that maps inputs to outputs.\n   * **Common Tasks:** Classification (categorizing data into classes) and Regression (predicting continuous numerical values).\n\n2. **Unsupervised Learning:**\n   * **Definition:** The algorithm is given unlabeled data and must find structure, patterns, or hidden relationships within the input data on its own.\n   * **Common Tasks:** Clustering (grouping similar data points together), dimensionality reduction, and association rule learning.\n\n3. **Reinforcement Learning (RL):**\n   * **Definition:** A computational approach where an agent takes actions in a dynamic environment to maximize some notion of cumulative reward through trial and error, guided by feedback from its actions.\n\n---\n\n### Core Concepts and Extensions\n\n* **Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs):** \n  Inspired by the structure and function of biological neural networks, neural networks form the backbone of modern predictive modeling. They consist of layers of interconnected nodes (neurons) that process data using weights that adjust during training.\n\n* **Deep Learning (DL):** \n  A specialized subfield of machine learning that focuses on utilizing **multilayered neural networks** (deep neural networks). Deep learning allows computational models to process complex, high-dimensional data—such as images, speech, and text—directly, performing automatic feature extraction without requiring manual feature engineering.\n\n* **Applications:** \n  Machine learning is widely used across a broad range of computing tasks where designing explicit algorithms is unfeasible. Prominent applications include computer vision, speech recognition, email filtering, medical diagnosis, natural language processing (NLP), and large language models (LLMs).','2026-11-10 13:19:26','2026-11-10 13:19:26',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(795,74,'user','Explain Machine Learning using Wikipedia sources','2026-11-10 13:44:30','2026-11-10 13:44:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(796,74,'assistant','Based on Wikipedia, **Machine Learning (ML)** is a subfield of artificial intelligence (AI) focused on the development and study of statistical algorithms that can learn from data and generalize to unseen data, allowing computers to perform tasks without being explicitly programmed. \n\nAn overview of machine learning, derived from Wikipedia\'s core documentation on the subject, covers several key aspects:\n\n### 1. Core Concept\nTraditionally, software is written by humans specifying exact rules and logic to transform input data into output. In contrast, machine learning flips this approach: developers feed algorithms **input data alongside corresponding answers (or training data)**, and the system uses statistical techniques to construct a mathematical model that can automatically find patterns, make predictions, or take decisions on new data.\n\n### 2. Connection to Other Fields\n* **Artificial Intelligence (AI):** Machine learning is a major subset of AI. While AI broadly encompasses any technique that enables computers to mimic human intelligence (including rule-based expert systems), ML specifically emphasizes *learning* from experience.\n* **Data Science & Statistics:** ML heavily overlaps with statistics, as both fields focus on analyzing data. However, machine learning places a greater emphasis on complex computational implementation, predictive accuracy, and algorithmic scaling.\n\n### 3. Main Types of Machine Learning Algorithms\nMachine learning approaches are traditionally categorized based on the nature of the \"signal\" or \"feedback\" available to the learning system:\n\n* **Supervised Learning:** The algorithm is presented with example inputs paired with desired outputs. The goal is to learn a general rule that maps inputs to outputs. Common tasks include *classification* (sorting data into categories) and *regression* (predicting continuous numerical values).\n* **Unsupervised Learning:** The algorithm is provided with unlabelled data, and the system is left to find structure, clusters, or hidden patterns on its own. Common tasks include *clustering* and *association rule learning*.\n* **Reinforcement Learning:** Inspired by behavioral psychology, an agent learns to make decisions by interacting with an environment to achieve a goal. It receives rewards or penalties for its actions, learning through trial-and-error to maximize its cumulative reward.\n\n### 4. Evolution and Deep Learning\nA prominent branch of machine learning is **Deep Learning (DL)**, which utilizes multi-layered artificial neural networks (ANNs) inspired loosely by biological brain structures. Deep learning models—such as transformers, large language models (LLMs), and diffusion models—have driven massive breakthroughs in complex domains like computer vision, speech recognition, and natural language processing by automatically learning hierarchical representations of data.\n\n***\n\n*Source references adapted from Wikipedia\'s entries on [Machine Learning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning) and the [Outline of Machine Learning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_machine_learning).*','2026-11-10 13:44:53','2026-11-10 13:44:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(797,74,'user','What is AI','2026-11-10 13:45:15','2026-11-10 13:45:15',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(798,75,'user','What is AI','2026-11-10 13:45:42','2026-11-10 13:45:42',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(799,75,'user','What is AI','2026-11-10 14:38:12','2026-11-10 14:38:12',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(800,75,'assistant','**Artificial Intelligence (AI)** refers to the simulation of human intelligence in machines programmed to think, learn, reason, perceive, and make decisions. \n\nAt its core, AI is a broad branch of computer science focused on building smart machines capable of performing tasks that typically require human cognitive functions.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what AI is, how it works, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. How AI Works\nTraditional computer software follows strict, pre-written rules and cannot adapt unless a programmer changes the code. AI, on the other hand, relies on advanced techniques to handle complex and dynamic data:\n* **Machine Learning (ML):** A subset of AI where computers are trained on massive amounts of data using algorithms to \"learn\" patterns and make predictions without being explicitly programmed for every single outcome.\n* **Deep Learning:** A specialized form of ML inspired by the human brain, using multi-layered artificial neural networks (ANNs) to process complex data like images, speech, and text.\n\n### 2. Common Types of AI\nAI is generally categorized by its capabilities:\n* **Narrow AI (Weak AI):** AI designed to perform a specific, dedicated task (e.g., facial recognition, internet search engines, virtual assistants like Siri or Alexa, or self-driving car navigation). *Almost all AI in use today falls into this category.*\n* **Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) (Strong AI):** A theoretical form of AI where a machine would possess the ability to understand, learn, and apply knowledge across any intellectual task just like a human. AGI does not yet exist.\n* **Artificial Superintelligence (ASI):** A hypothetical future stage where AI would surpass human intelligence across all domains.\n\n### 3. Key Subfields of AI\n* **Natural Language Processing (NLP):** Enables machines to understand, interpret, and generate human language (e.g., translation tools, chatbots, and large language models).\n* **Computer Vision:** Allows computers to \"see\" and interpret visual data from the world, such as videos and images.\n* **Robotics:** Combines AI with mechanical engineering to create machines that can physical interact with their environment (e.g., automated manufacturing arms or warehouse robots).\n\n### 4. Where Is AI Used Today?\nAI is embedded in many everyday technologies:\n* **Everyday Apps:** Recommendation algorithms on Netflix or Spotify, predictive text on your phone, and fraud detection on credit cards.\n* **Healthcare:** Analyzing medical scans (like X-rays and MRIs) for early disease detection, drug discovery, and personalized treatment plans.\n* **Business & Finance:** Automated customer service, algorithmic trading, and supply chain optimization.\n* **Creativity:** Generative AI tools that can write text, compose music, generate images, and write computer code.\n\nIn short, AI is transforming the way we live, work, and solve problems by augmenting human capabilities with machine speed and scale.','2026-11-10 14:38:21','2026-11-10 14:38:21',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(801,76,'user','What is the difference between AI and machine learning?','2026-11-11 13:08:13','2026-11-11 13:08:13',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(802,76,'assistant','To understand the difference between Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML), it helps to think of them in terms of a hierarchy: **AI is the broad concept, and Machine Learning is a specific technique used to achieve it.**\n\nHere is the breakdown of how they differ and how they relate.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Artificial Intelligence (The \"Big Picture\")\nAI is the overarching field of computer science dedicated to creating systems capable of performing tasks that typically require human intelligence.\n\n*   **Goal:** To build machines that can \"think,\" reason, solve problems, make decisions, or understand language.\n*   **Scope:** It is a very broad umbrella. It covers everything from a simple chess program that follows a set of hard-coded rules to complex systems that can drive cars.\n*   **Analogy:** If AI were a vehicle, it would be the entire concept of \"transportation.\"\n\n### 2. Machine Learning (The \"How-To\")\nMachine Learning is a specific subset of AI. Instead of programming a computer with explicit, step-by-step rules to perform a task, you \"train\" it using data.\n\n*   **Goal:** To allow computers to learn from experience (data) so they can improve their performance on a specific task over time without being explicitly programmed for every scenario.\n*   **How it works:** You feed the computer vast amounts of data, and it uses statistical algorithms to find patterns. It then uses those patterns to make predictions or decisions about new, unseen data.\n*   **Analogy:** If AI is \"transportation,\" then Machine Learning is a specific type of engine that powers some of those vehicles.\n\n---\n\n### Key Differences at a Glance\n\n| Feature | Artificial Intelligence (AI) | Machine Learning (ML) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Definition** | The broad science of mimicking human intelligence. | The science of getting computers to learn from data. |\n| **Relationship** | The parent category. | A subset (a tool) within AI. |\n| **Approach** | Can be rule-based (if/then) or learning-based. | Entirely data-driven. |\n| **Objective** | To create intelligent systems. | To allow systems to learn and improve from data. |\n\n---\n\n### A Simple Example\nImagine you want to build a system that identifies emails as \"Spam\" or \"Not Spam\":\n\n*   **The \"Old\" AI way (Rule-based):** You write a list of hard-coded rules: \"If the email contains the word \'Winner,\' mark as spam. If it contains the word \'Free,\' mark as spam.\" This is AI, but it is **not** Machine Learning, because the computer isn\'t learning; it\'s just following your instructions.\n*   **The Machine Learning way:** You show the computer 10,000 emails already labeled as \"Spam\" or \"Not Spam.\" The computer analyzes them, identifies patterns (like specific sender addresses, word frequencies, or link structures), and builds its own model to classify future emails. This is **Machine Learning.**\n\n### Summary\n*   **AI** is the goal (creating smart machines).\n*   **Machine Learning** is the method (teaching machines by feeding them data).\n\n*Note: You might also hear the term **Deep Learning**. This is a specialized, more advanced subset of Machine Learning that uses \"neural networks\" (modeled after the human brain) to handle very complex tasks like image recognition or voice translation.*','2026-11-11 13:08:17','2026-11-11 13:08:17',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(803,76,'user','Write a simple JavaScript function to reverse a string.','2026-11-11 13:09:03','2026-11-11 13:09:03',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(804,76,'assistant','Here is a simple and common way to reverse a string in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nfunction reverseString(str) {\n    // 1. Split the string into an array of characters\n    // 2. Reverse the array\n    // 3. Join the array back into a string\n    return str.split(\"\").reverse().join(\"\");\n}\n\n// Example usage:\nconsole.log(reverseString(\"hello\")); // Output: \"olleh\"\n```\n\n### How it works:\n1.  **`.split(\"\")`**: This takes the string (e.g., `\"hello\"`) and turns it into an array of individual characters: `[\"h\", \"e\", \"l\", \"l\", \"o\"]`.\n2.  **`.reverse()`**: This is a built-in array method that flips the order of the elements: `[\"o\", \"l\", \"l\", \"e\", \"h\"]`.\n3.  **`.join(\"\")`**: This takes the array elements and stitches them back together into a single string: `\"olleh\"`.','2026-11-11 13:09:18','2026-11-11 13:09:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(805,76,'user','What is the main topic of the uploaded document?','2026-11-11 13:09:38','2026-11-11 13:09:38',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(806,76,'assistant','It appears that no documents have been uploaded to this session. Please upload a document, a