860,80,'user','List all the certificates mention in the resume','2026-11-11 15:34:52','2026-11-11 15:34:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(861,80,'assistant','The certificates mentioned in the resume are:\n\n*   **JavaScript Full Stack / Web Development** — NAVTTC\n*   **AI Fundamentals: Foundations for Understanding AI** — Cisco / IBM SkillsBuild, 2026\n*   **Advanced Certification in JavaScript & Full Stack Development (MEAN-MERN)** — Centre for Digital Literacy & Business Management (September 2025 – November 2025; Grade: A+)','2026-11-11 15:35:01','2026-11-11 15:35:01',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','null',0),(897,87,'user','What is Artificial Intelligence? Explain with simple examples.','2026-11-12 13:51:37','2026-11-12 13:51:37',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(898,87,'assistant','At its simplest, **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** is a field of computer science that aims to build machines or software capable of performing tasks that typically require human intelligence.\n\nThink of it this way: Normally, computers are like calculators—they only do exactly what you program them to do, step by step. AI, on the other hand, is designed to **learn, reason, and solve problems** on its own by looking at vast amounts of information.\n\nHere are a few simple examples to help visualize how it works:\n\n### 1. Recommendation Systems (The \"Guessing\" AI)\nHave you ever noticed how Netflix suggests movies you might like, or how Amazon suggests products?\n*   **How it works:** The AI looks at what you have watched or bought in the past, compares it to millions of other users who have similar tastes, and \"predicts\" what you will enjoy next. It learns your preferences over time.\n\n### 2. Virtual Assistants (The \"Understanding\" AI)\nTools like Siri, Alexa, or Google Assistant use AI to understand human language.\n*   **How it works:** When you speak, the AI doesn\'t just look for keywords. It uses \"Natural Language Processing\" to understand the *intent* behind your sentence. If you ask, \"What\'s the weather like?\", it recognizes that you are asking for a weather report for your current location, even though you didn\'t say the city name.\n\n### 3. Image Recognition (The \"Seeing\" AI)\nThink about how your phone can automatically organize your photos into folders like \"Cats,\" \"Beaches,\" or \"People.\"\n*   **How it works:** The AI has been shown millions of photos of cats. By studying those patterns—the shape of the ears, the whiskers, the fur—it learns to identify a cat in a new photo it has never seen before.\n\n### 4. Navigation Apps (The \"Planning\" AI)\nApps like Google Maps or Waze use AI to get you from point A to point B.\n*   **How it works:** It doesn\'t just look at a map; it analyzes live data from thousands of other drivers. It identifies traffic jams, construction, or accidents in real-time and calculates the fastest possible route, constantly adjusting as conditions change.\n\n---\n\n### The Big Picture: How do they learn?\nMost modern AI uses something called **Machine Learning**. Instead of a human writing a rule for every possible scenario (which would be impossible), we give the computer a \"model\" and feed it massive amounts of data. \n\n*   **Example:** If you want to teach a computer to recognize a dog, you don\'t write a rule saying \"if it has floppy ears and a tail, it\'s a dog.\" Instead, you show the computer 100,000 photos of dogs and 100,000 photos of things that aren\'t dogs. The computer finds the patterns itself.\n\n**In short:** AI is just a tool that helps computers make smart decisions based on patterns they find in data, rather than just following a list of rigid instructions.','2026-11-12 13:51:45','2026-11-12 13:51:45',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"At its simplest, **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** is a field of computer science that aims to build machines or software capable of performing tasks that typically require human intelligence.\\n\\nThink of it this way: Normally, computers are like calculators—they only do exactly what you program them to do, step by step. AI, on the other hand, is designed to **learn, reason, and solve problems** on its own by looking at vast amounts of information.\\n\\nHere are a few simple examples to help visualize how it works:\\n\\n### 1. Recommendation Systems (The \\\"Guessing\\\" AI)\\nHave you ever noticed how Netflix suggests movies you might like, or how Amazon suggests products?\\n*   **How it works:** The AI looks at what you have watched or bought in the past, compares it to millions of other users who have similar tastes, and \\\"predicts\\\" what you will enjoy next. It learns your preferences over time.\\n\\n### 2. Virtual Assistants (The \\\"Understanding\\\" AI)\\nTools like Siri, Alexa, or Google Assistant use AI to understand human language.\\n*   **How it works:** When you speak, the AI doesn\'t just look for keywords. It uses \\\"Natural Language Processing\\\" to understand the *intent* behind your sentence. If you ask, \\\"What\'s the weather like?\\\", it recognizes that you are asking for a weather report for your current location, even though you didn\'t say the city name.\\n\\n### 3. Image Recognition (The \\\"Seeing\\\" AI)\\nThink about how your phone can automatically organize your photos into folders like \\\"Cats,\\\" \\\"Beaches,\\\" or \\\"People.\\\"\\n*   **How it works:** The AI has been shown millions of photos of cats. By studying those patterns—the shape of the ears, the whiskers, the fur—it learns to identify a cat in a new photo it has never seen before.\\n\\n### 4. Navigation Apps (The \\\"Planning\\\" AI)\\nApps like Google Maps or Waze use AI to get you from point A to point B.\\n*   **How it works:** It doesn\'t just look at a map; it analyzes live data from thousands of other drivers. It identifies traffic jams, construction, or accidents in real-time and calculates the fastest possible route, constantly adjusting as conditions change.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The Big Picture: How do they learn?\\nMost modern AI uses something called **Machine Learning**. Instead of a human writing a rule for every possible scenario (which would be impossible), we give the computer a \\\"model\\\" and feed it massive amounts of data. \\n\\n*   **Example:** If you want to teach a computer to recognize a dog, you don\'t write a rule saying \\\"if it has floppy ears and a tail, it\'s a dog.\\\" Instead, you show the computer 100,000 photos of dogs and 100,000 photos of things that aren\'t dogs. The computer finds the patterns itself.\\n\\n**In short:** AI is just a tool that helps computers make smart decisions based on patterns they find in data, rather than just following a list of rigid instructions.\"]',0),(901,88,'user','Tell me about Albert Einstein using Wikipedia.','2026-11-12 13:54:13','2026-11-12 13:54:13',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(902,88,'assistant','Albert Einstein (March 14, 1879 – April 18, 1955) was a German-born theoretical physicist who is widely considered one of the most influential scientists of all time.\n\nHere are some key points about his life and work:\n\n*   **Theory of Relativity:** He is best known for developing the theory of relativity, which fundamentally changed our understanding of space, time, and gravity.\n*   **Scientific Impact:** His work had a profound impact on modern physics. Beyond relativity, he made significant contributions to the development of quantum theory.\n*   **Life:** Born in Germany, he later lived and worked in various countries, eventually settling in the United States, where he lived in Princeton, New Jersey, for the latter part of his life.\n*   **Other Interests:** Einstein was also known for his views on philosophy, religion, and politics, which have been the subject of extensive study and discussion.\n\nIf you would like to know more about a specific aspect of his life, such as his scientific discoveries, his personal history, or his political views, feel free to ask!\n\n### Sources\n[1] [Albert Einstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Einstein)\n[2] [Hans Albert Einstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Albert_Einstein)\n[3] [Einstein family](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Einstein_family)\n[4] [Albert Brooks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Brooks)\n[5] [Albert Einstein College of Medicine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Einstein_College_of_Medicine)\n[6] [Brain of Albert Einstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brain_of_Albert_Einstein)\n[7] [Religious and philosophical views of Albert Einstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religious_and_philosophical_views_of_Albert_Einstein)\n[8] [Mileva Marić](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mileva_Mari%C4%87)\n[9] [Albert Einstein House](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Einstein_House)\n[10] [Political views of Albert Einstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_views_of_Albert_Einstein)','2026-11-12 13:54:18','2026-11-12 13:54:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"Albert Einstein (March 14, 1879 – April 18, 1955) was a German-born theoretical physicist who is widely considered one of the most influential scientists of all time.\\n\\nHere are some key points about his life and work:\\n\\n*   **Theory of Relativity:** He is best known for developing the theory of relativity, which fundamentally changed our understanding of space, time, and gravity.\\n*   **Scientific Impact:** His work had a profound impact on modern physics. Beyond relativity, he made significant contributions to the development of quantum theory.\\n*   **Life:** Born in Germany, he later lived and worked in various countries, eventually settling in the United States, where he lived in Princeton, New Jersey, for the latter part of his life.\\n*   **Other Interests:** Einstein was also known for his views on philosophy, religion, and politics, which have been the subject of extensive study and discussion.\\n\\nIf you would like to know more about a specific aspect of his life, such as his scient