stics (completed/pending items) and integration of Multer for profile image uploads.\\n\\n4.  **ResumEase — AI-Powered HR Assistant**\\n    *   **Technologies:** Python, Django, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5, JavaScript, SQLite, AI/ML\\n    *   **Features:** Resume screening, interview scheduling, resume uploading, candidate information extraction, skill-matching, candidate shortlisting, and responsive dashboards.\"]',0),(986,106,'user','What are EDA techniques?','2026-11-16 08:13:03','2026-11-16 08:13:03',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(987,106,'assistant','Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) techniques are categorized by the number of variables being analyzed at one time:\n\n*   **Univariate Analysis:** Used to analyze one variable at a time to understand its characteristics and distribution.\n    *   **Common Techniques:** Mean, median, mode, minimum and maximum values, standard deviation, frequency counts, histograms, and box plots.\n    *   **Example:** Finding the average age of students in a university.\n\n*   **Bivariate Analysis:** Used to analyze two variables to understand their relationships or differences.\n    *   **Common Techniques:** Correlation, cross-tabulation, scatter plots, line graphs, group comparisons, and comparing averages between categories.\n    *   **Example:** Checking whether older students get higher marks.\n\n*   **Multivariate Analysis:** Used to analyze two or more variables together to understand more complex relationships.\n    *   **Common Techniques:** Correlation matrix, pair plots, multiple variable visualization, Principal Component Analysis (PCA), spatial analysis, and cluster analysis.\n    *   **Example:** Analyzing age, experience, education, and salary together to understand employee salary.\n\nAdditionally, as part of the overall EDA process, other important steps and techniques include **outlier detection**, **hypothesis testing**, **data visualization**, and **feature engineering**.','2026-11-16 08:13:07','2026-11-16 08:13:07',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) techniques are categorized by the number of variables being analyzed at one time:\\n\\n*   **Univariate Analysis:** Used to analyze one variable at a time to understand its characteristics and distribution.\\n    *   **Common Techniques:** Mean, median, mode, minimum and maximum values, standard deviation, frequency counts, histograms, and box plots.\\n    *   **Example:** Finding the average age of students in a university.\\n\\n*   **Bivariate Analysis:** Used to analyze two variables to understand their relationships or differences.\\n    *   **Common Techniques:** Correlation, cross-tabulation, scatter plots, line graphs, group comparisons, and comparing averages between categories.\\n    *   **Example:** Checking whether older students get higher marks.\\n\\n*   **Multivariate Analysis:** Used to analyze two or more variables together to understand more complex relationships.\\n    *   **Common Techniques:** Correlation matrix, pair plots, multiple variable visualization, Principal Component Analysis (PCA), spatial analysis, and cluster analysis.\\n    *   **Example:** Analyzing age, experience, education, and salary together to understand employee salary.\\n\\nAdditionally, as part of the overall EDA process, other important steps and techniques include **outlier detection**, **hypothesis testing**, **data visualization**, and **feature engineering**.\"]',0),(988,106,'user','is the above answer is from my uploaded document?','2026-11-16 08:15:05','2026-11-16 08:15:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(989,106,'assistant','No, the answer provided about EDA techniques is based on general knowledge. It was not sourced from your uploaded documents.','2026-11-16 08:15:07','2026-11-16 08:15:07',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"No, the answer provided about EDA techniques is based on general knowledge. It was not sourced from your uploaded documents.\"]',0),(990,107,'user','What are EDA techniques?','2026-11-16 08:18:31','2026-11-16 08:18:31',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(991,107,'assistant','Based on the provided document, Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) techniques are categorized by the number of variables being analyzed:\n\n*   **Univariate Analysis:** Analyzes one variable at a time to understand its characteristics and distribution. Common techniques include calculating the mean, median, mode, minimum and maximum values, standard deviation, and frequency count, as well as using histograms and box plots.\n*   **Bivariate Analysis:** Analyzes two variables to understand their relationships or differences. Common techniques include correlation, cross-tabulation, scatter plots, line graphs, group comparisons, and comparing averages between categories.\n*   **Multivariate Analysis:** Analyzes two or more variables together to understand complex relations. Common techniques include correlation matrices, pair plots, multiple variable visualization, principal component analysis (PCA), spatial analysis, and cluster analysis.\n\nThe document (filename: *EDA (1).pdf*) also lists a broader set of final steps for performing EDA, which includes data cleaning, finding and handling missing values, outlier detection, feature engineering, hypothesis testing, and data visualization.','2026-11-16 08:19:58','2026-11-16 08:19:58',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"Based on the provided document, Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) techniques are categorized by the number of variables being analyzed:\\n\\n*   **Univariate Analysis:** Analyzes one variable at a time to understand its characteristics and distribution. Common techniques include calculating the mean, median, mode, minimum and maximum values, standard deviation, and frequency count, as well as using histograms and box plots.\\n*   **Bivariate Analysis:** Analyzes two variables to understand their relationships or differences. Common techniques include correlation, cross-tabulation, scatter plots, line graphs, group comparisons, and comparing averages between categories.\\n*   **Multivariate Analysis:** Analyzes two or more variables together to understand complex relations. Common techniques include correlation matrices, pair plots, multiple variable visualization, principal component analysis (PCA), spatial analysis, and cluster analysis.\\n\\nThe document (filename: *EDA (1).pdf*) also lists a broader set of final steps for performing EDA, which includes data cleaning, finding and handling missing values, outlier detection, feature engineering, hypothesis testing, and data visualization.\"]',0),(992,107,'user','Explain REST API vs GraphQL','2026-11-16 08:21:05','2026-11-16 08:21:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(993,107,'assistant','REST (Representational State Transfer) and GraphQL are both ways for different software applications to communicate with each other over the internet, but they approach data retrieval differently.\n\nHere is a breakdown of the key differences:\n\n### 1. Data Fetching\n*   **REST:** Uses fixed endpoints (URLs) for specific resources. Each endpoint typically returns a fixed structure of data. If you need data from multiple resources (e.g., a user\'s profile and their latest posts), you often have to make multiple requests to different endpoints.\n*   **GraphQL:** Uses a single endpoint. Clients send a query that specifies exactly what data they need. The server returns exactly that data in a single response, avoiding the \"over-fetching\" or \"under-fetching\" of data.\n\n### 2. Flexibility and Efficiency\n*   **REST:** Can lead to **over-fetching** (getting more data than you need, which wastes bandwidth) or **under-fetching** (not getting enough data, requiring a second request).\n*   **GraphQL:** Highly efficient because the client controls exactly what fields are returned. You get exactly what you ask for, and nothing more.\n\n### 3. Versioning\n*   **REST:** Often requires versioning the API (e.g., `/api/v1/users` vs `/api/v2/users`) when the data structure changes, because changing an existing endpoint might break older clients.\n*   **GraphQL:** Generally avoids versioning. Because clients request specific fields, you can add new fields to the server without breaking existing queries from older clients. You can simply deprecate old fields over time.\n\n### 4. Learning Curve and Complexity\n*   **REST:** Generally easier to understand and implement initially. It leverages standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and works well with browser caching.\n*   **GraphQL:** Has a steeper learning curve. Setting up the server-side \"schema\" and \"resolvers\" (the code that fetches the data for each field) is more complex than setting up a standard REST endpoint.\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Feature | REST | GraphQL |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Endpoint** | Multiple endpoints (resources) | Single endpoint |\n| **Data Fetching** | Fixed structure; can over/under-fetch | Client-defined; exact data returned |\n| **Versioning** | Often requires versioning (v1, v2) | Rarely needs versioning |\n| **Complexity** | Simple to start | More complex setup |\n| **Caching** | Easy (built-in to HTTP) | Harder (requires custom solutions) |\n\n**Which one should you choose?**\n*   **Use REST if:** You need a simple, standard API, have a well-defined resource-based architecture, or rely heavily on standard HTTP caching.\n*   **Use GraphQL if:** You have a complex application with many interconnected data sources, you want to optimize for performance on mobile devices (by reducing data transfer), or you have multiple client teams (web, iOS, Android) that need to request data differently.\n\n### Sources\n[1] [Ballerina (programming language)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballerina_(programming_language))\n[2] [Headless commerce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headless_commerce)\n[3] [Dynamic web page](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_web_page)\n[4] [Server application programming interface](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Server_application_programming_interface)\n[5] [Front end and back end](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Front_end_and_back_end)\n[6] [Headless content management system](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headless_content_management_system)\n[7] [Icinga](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icinga)\n[8] [Backend as a service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backend_as_a_service)\n[9] [Underfloor heating](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Underfloor_heating)','2026-11-16 08:21:12','2026-11-16 08:21:12',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"REST (Representational State Transfer) and GraphQL are both ways for different software applications to communicate with each other over the internet, but they approach data retrieval differently.\\n\\nHere is a breakdown of the key differences:\\n\\n### 1. Data Fetching\\n*   **REST:** Uses fixed endpoints (URLs) for specific resources. Each endpoint typically returns a fixed structure of data. If you need data from multiple resources (e.g., a user\'s profile and their latest posts), you often have to make multiple requests to different endpoints.\\n*   **GraphQL:** Uses a single endpoint. Clients send a query that specifies exactly what data they need. The server returns exactly that data in a single response, avoiding the \\\"over-fetching\\\" or \\\"under-fetching\\\" of data.\\n\\n### 2. Flexibility and Efficiency\\n*   **REST:** Can lead to **over-fetching** (getting more data than you need, which wastes bandwidth) or **under-fetching** (not getting enough data, requiring a second request).\\n*   **GraphQL:** Highly efficient because the client controls exactly what fields are returned. You get exactly what you ask for, and nothing more.\\n\\n### 3. Versioning\\n*   **REST:** Often requires versioning the API (e.g., `/api/v1/users` vs `/api/v2/users`) when the data structure changes, because changing an existing endpoint might break older clients.\\n*   **GraphQL:** Generally avoids versioning. Because clients request specific fields, you can add new fields to the server without breaking existing queries from older clients. You can simply deprecate old fields over time.\\n\\n### 4. Learning Curve and Complexity\\n*   **REST:** Generally easier to understand and implement initially. It leverages standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and works well with browser caching.\\n*   **GraphQL:** Has a steeper learning curve. Setting up the server-side \\\"schema\\\" and \\\"resolvers\\\" (the code that fetches the data for each field) is more complex than setting up a standard REST endpoint.\\n\\n### Summary Table\\n\\n| Feature | REST | GraphQL |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Endpoint** | Multiple endpoints (resources) | Single endpoint |\\n| **Data Fetching** | Fixed structure; can over/under-fetch | Client-defined; exact data returned |\\n| **Versioning** | Often requires versioning (v1, v2) | Rarely needs versioning |\\n| **Complexity** | Simple to start | More complex setup |\\n| **Caching** | Easy (built-in to HTTP) | Harder (requires custom solutions) |\\n\\n**Which one should you choose?**\\n*   **Use REST if:** You need a simple, standard API, have a well-defined resource-based architecture, or rely heavily on standard HTTP caching.\\n*   **Use GraphQL if:** You have a complex application with many interconnected data sources, you want to optimize for performance on mobile devices (by reducing data transfer), or you have multiple client teams (web, iOS, Android) that need to request data differently.\\n\\n### Sources\\n[1] [Ballerina (programming language)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballerina_(programming_language))\\n[2] [Headless commerce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headless_commerce)\\n[3] [Dynamic web page](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_web_page)\\n[4] [Server application programming interface](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Server_application_programming_interface)\\n[5] [Front end and back end](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Front_end_and_back_end)\\n[6] [Headless content management system](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headless_content_management_system)\\n[7] [Icinga](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icinga)\\n[8] [Backend as a service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backend_as_a_service)\\n[9] [Underfloor heating](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Underfloor_heating)\"]',0),(994,107,'user','What is EDA?','2026-11-16 08:38:46','2026-11-16 08:38:46',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(995,107,'assistant','[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)**\\n\\nAccording to the uploaded document (filename: *EDA (1).pdf*), EDA is:\\n\\n* **A process** of examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset before it is used for further analysis or for building a machine‑learning model.\\n* **Purpose:** To explore the data to discover what it contains, find problems (such as missing values or outliers), uncover patterns, and learn what the data is telling you.\\n\\nThe document lists several concrete goals of EDA:\\n\\n- Determine **what data you have** (rows, columns, data types, etc.).\\n- Verify that the data is **correct and complete** (identify missing or duplicate values).\\n- Understand the **distribution** of each variable and detect **outliers**.\\n- Discover **relationships** between columns (e.g., correlations).\\n- Prepare the data for downstream tasks (feature engineering, modeling, etc.).\\n\\nTypical applications mentioned include market analysis, risk assessment, quality control, and healthcare data analysis.\\n\"}]','2026-11-16 08:38:50','2026-11-16 08:38:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)**\\\\n\\\\nAccording to the uploaded document (filename: *EDA (1).pdf*), EDA is:\\\\n\\\\n* **A process** of examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset before it is used for further analysis or for building a machine‑learning model.\\\\n* **Purpose:** To explore the data to discover what it contains, find problems (such as missing values or outliers), uncover patterns, and learn what the data is telling you.\\\\n\\\\nThe document lists several concrete goals of EDA:\\\\n\\\\n- Determine **what data you have** (rows, columns, data types, etc.).\\\\n- Verify that the data is **correct and complete** (identify missing or duplicate values).\\\\n- Understand the **distribution** of each variable and detect **outliers**.\\\\n- Discover **relationships** between columns (e.g., correlations).\\\\n- Prepare the data for downstream tasks (feature engineering, modeling, etc.).\\\\n\\\\nTypical applications mentioned include market analysis, risk assessment, quality control, and healthcare data analysis.\\\\n\\\"}]\"]',0),(996,108,'user','What is EDA?','2026-11-16 08:46:33','2026-11-16 08:46:33',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(997,108,'assistant','**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** is the process of **examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset** before it is used for further analysis or for building a machine‑learning model.  \n\nAccording to the document **“EDA (1).pdf”**, EDA involves exploring the data to:\n\n* Discover patterns, missing values, outliers, and relationships  \n* Assess data quality and distribution  \n* Gain a clear picture of what the data contains and how it behaves  \n\nIn short, EDA is the initial step that helps you understand the data you have, verify its correctness, and decide what to do next before any modeling or deeper analysis.','2026-11-16 08:49:29','2026-11-16 08:49:29',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** is the process of **examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset** before it is used for further analysis or for building a machine‑learning model.  \\n\\nAccording to the document **“EDA (1).pdf”**, EDA involves exploring the data to:\\n\\n* Discover patterns, missing values, outliers, and relationships  \\n* Assess data quality and distribution  \\n* Gain a clear picture of what the data contains and how it behaves  \\n\\nIn short, EDA is the initial step that helps you understand the data you have, verify its correctness, and decide what to do next before any modeling or deeper analysis.\"]',0),(998,109,'user','Explain the difference between AI and agentic AI','2026-11-16 08:53:40','2026-11-16 08:53:40',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(999,109,'assistant','To understand the difference between standard AI and \"agentic\" AI, it helps to think about the difference between a **tool** and an **employee**.\n\n### 1. Standard AI (The \"Tool\" Model)\nStandard AI—like a basic chatbot or a predictive model—is designed to perform a specific task when prompted by a human. It follows a direct instruction, processes the information provided, and gives an output.\n\n*   **Reactive:** It only acts when a human gives it a command.\n*   **Linear:** It completes the task as requested and then stops.\n*   **Static:** It does not usually \"think\" about what to do next or how to improve the process; it simply executes the input.\n*   **Example:** You ask a standard AI to \"write an email about a meeting.\" It writes the email, and then waits for your next instruction.\n\n### 2. Agentic AI (The \"Employee\" Model)\nAgentic AI (or an \"AI Agent\") is designed to be autonomous. Instead of just completing a single task, it is given a **goal**. It has the ability to reason, plan, and use tools to achieve that goal without needing human intervention at every step.\n\n*   **Proactive:** You give it a high-level objective (e.g., \"Find a flight to London under $800 and book it\"), and it figures out the steps to get there.\n*   **Iterative:** It can break the goal into smaller tasks, assess its own progress, and adjust if it hits a roadblock.\n*   **Tool-Using:** It can interact with other software (browsers, calendars, email, databases) to perform actions on your behalf.\n*   **Example:** You tell an AI Agent to \"organize a travel itinerary.\" It will search for flights, check your calendar for availability, compare hotel prices, and perhaps even draft an email to your boss requesting the time off—all without you telling it how to do each individual step.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison Table\n\n| Feature | Standard AI | Agentic AI |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Primary Interaction** | Responds to prompts. | Responds to goals/objectives. |\n| **Autonomy** | Low; requires constant input. | High; operates independently. |\n| **Workflow** | Linear (Input $\\rightarrow$ Output). | Iterative (Plan $\\rightarrow$ Act $\\rightarrow$ Evaluate). |\n| **Capabilities** | Generates content/analysis. | Executes tasks and uses external tools. |\n| **Focus** | \"What do you want me to write?\" | \"How can I solve this problem?\" |\n\n### Summary\n*   **Standard AI** is like a **calculator or a word processor**: You provide the input, and it provides the result. It is excellent for productivity and creation.\n*   **Agentic AI** is like a **personal assistant**: You give it a mission, and it manages the details, navigates obstacles, and uses the necessary tools to get the job done. \n\nIn short, while standard AI is about **generating information**, agentic AI is about **taking action.**','2026-11-16 08:53:44','2026-11-16 08:53:44',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"To understand the difference between standard AI and \\\"agentic\\\" AI, it helps to think about the difference between a **tool** and an **employee**.\\n\\n### 1. Standard AI (The \\\"Tool\\\" Model)\\nStandard AI—like a basic chatbot or a predictive model—is designed to perform a specific task when prompted by a human. It follows a direct instruction, processes the information provided, and gives an output.\\n\\n*   **Reactive:** It only acts when a human gives it a command.\\n*   **Linear:** It completes the task as requested and then stops.\\n*   **Static:** It does not usually \\\"think\\\" about what to do next or how to improve the process; it simply executes the input.\\n*   **Example:** You ask a standard AI to \\\"write an email about a meeting.\\\" It writes the email, and then waits for your next instruction.\\n\\n### 2. Agentic AI (The \\\"Employee\\\" Model)\\nAgentic AI (or an \\\"AI Agent\\\") is designed to be autonomous. Instead of just completing a single task, it is given a **goal**. It has the ability to reason, plan, and use tools to achieve that goal without needing human intervention at every step.\\n\\n*   **Proactive:** You give it a high-level objective (e.g., \\\"Find a flight to London under $800 and book it\\\"), and it figures out the steps to get there.\\n*   **Iterative:** It can break the goal into smaller tasks, assess its own progress, and adjust if it hits a roadblock.\\n*   **Tool-Using:** It can interact with other software (browsers, calendars, email, databases) to perform actions on your behalf.\\n*   **Example:** You tell an AI Agent to \\\"organize a travel itinerary.\\\" It will search for flights, check your calendar for availability, compare hotel prices, and perhaps even draft an email to your boss requesting the time off—all without you telling it how to do each individual step.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Comparison Table\\n\\n| Feature | Standard AI | Agentic AI |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Primary Interaction** | Responds to prompts. | Responds to goals/objectives. |\\n| **Autonomy** | Low; requires constant input. | High; operates independently. |\\n| **Workflow** | Linear (Input $\\\\rightarrow$ Output). | Iterative (Plan $\\\\rightarrow$ Act $\\\\rightarrow$ Evaluate). |\\n| **Capabilities** | Generates content/analysis. | Executes tasks and uses external tools. |\\n| **Focus** | \\\"What do you want me to write?\\\" | \\\"How can I solve this problem?\\\" |\\n\\n### Summary\\n*   **Standard AI** is like a **calculator or a word processor**: You provide the input, and it provides the result. It is excellent for productivity and creation.\\n*   **Agentic AI** is like a **personal assistant**: You give it a mission, and it manages the details, navigates obstacles, and uses the necessary tools to get the job done. \\n\\nIn short, while standard AI is about **generating information**, agentic AI is about **taking action.**\"]',0),(1000,109,'user','What is EDa and it\'s technique','2026-11-16 08:54:07','2026-11-16 08:54:07',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(1001,109,'assistant','Based on the provided documents, Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is the process of examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset before using it for further analysis or building a machine learning model.\n\n### Purpose of EDA\nEDA helps you \"get to know\" your data. Its primary purposes include:\n*   **Understanding the data:** Identifying rows, columns, data types, and what each column represents.\n*   **Checking data quality:** Finding missing values, duplicate values, and potential outliers.\n*   **Discovering patterns and relationships:** Seeing how variables are distributed and how they relate to one another.\n*   **Preparing for next steps:** Deciding what to do next with the data.\n\n### EDA Techniques\nEDA is categorized based on the number of variables being analyzed:\n\n#### 1. Univariate Analysis (One variable at a time)\nUsed to understand the characteristics of a single variable, such as its typical value, spread, and distribution.\n*   **Common Techniques:** Mean, median, mode, minimum and maximum values, standard deviation, frequency counts, histograms, and box plots.\n*   **Example:** Calculating the average age of passengers in a dataset.\n\n#### 2. Bivariate Analysis (Two variables at a time)\nUsed to understand the relationships or differences between two variables.\n*   **Common Techniques:** Correlation, cross-tabulation, scatter plots, line graphs, group comparisons, and comparing averages between categories.\n*   **Example:** Checking if older students in a university tend to receive higher marks.\n\n#### 3. Multivariate Analysis (Two or more variables)\nUsed to understand more complex relationships involving multiple variables.\n*   **Common Techniques:** Correlation matrix, pair plots, multiple-variable visualization, principal component analysis (PCA), spatial analysis, and cluster analysis.\n*   **Example:** Analyzing age, experience, education, and salary together to understand employee compensation.\n\n### Quick Guide for Choosing a Technique\nAccording to the *EDA_Techniques_Tables.pdf*, you should choose a technique based on the question you want to answer:\n\n| Question | Technique(s) to use |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| What is the average/middle/most common value? | Mean / Median / Mode |\n| How much do values vary? | Standard deviation / IQR |\n| How is a numerical feature distributed? | Histogram / Box plot |\n| Are there potential outliers? | Box plot / IQR / Z-score |\n| How are two numerical features related? | Correlation / Scatter plot |\n| How do groups differ? | Group comparison / Average by category |\n\n*Sources: \"EDA (1).pdf\" and \"EDA_Techniques_Tables.pdf\"*','2026-11-16 08:54:17','2026-11-16 08:54:17',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"Based on the provided documents, Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is the process of examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset before using it for further analysis or building a machine learning model.\\n\\n### Purpose of EDA\\nEDA helps you \\\"get to know\\\" your data. Its primary purposes include:\\n*   **Understanding the data:** Identifying rows, columns, data types, and what each column represents.\\n*   **Checking data quality:** Finding missing values, duplicate values, and potential outliers.\\n*   **Discovering patterns and relationships:** Seeing how variables are distributed and how they relate to one another.\\n*   **Preparing for next steps:** Deciding what to do next with the data.\\n\\n### EDA Techniques\\nEDA is categorized based on the number of variables being analyzed:\\n\\n#### 1. Univariate Analysis (One variable at a time)\\nUsed to understand the characteristics of a single variable, such as its typical value, spread, and distribution.\\n*   **Common Techniques:** Mean, median, mode, minimum and maximum values, standard deviation, frequency counts, histograms, and box plots.\\n*   **Example:** Calculating the average age of passengers in a dataset.\\n\\n#### 2. Bivariate Analysis (Two variables at a time)\\nUsed to understand the relationships or differences between two variables.\\n*   **Common Techniques:** Correlation, cross-tabulation, scatter plots, line graphs, group comparisons, and comparing averages between categories.\\n*   **Example:** Checking if older students in a university tend to receive higher marks.\\n\\n#### 3. Multivariate Analysis (Two or more variables)\\nUsed to understand more complex relationships involving multiple variables.\\n*   **Common Techniques:** Correlation matrix, pair plots, multiple-variable visualization, principal component analysis (PCA), spatial analysis, and cluster analysis.\\n*   **Example:** Analyzing age, experience, education, and salary together to understand employee compensation.\\n\\n### Quick Guide for Choosing a Technique\\nAccording to the *EDA_Techniques_Tables.pdf*, you should choose a technique based on the question you want to answer:\\n\\n| Question | Technique(s) to use |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| What is the average/middle/most common value? | Mean / Median / Mode |\\n| How much do values vary? | Standard deviation / IQR |\\n| How is a numerical feature distributed? | Histogram / Box plot |\\n| Are there potential outliers? | Box plot / IQR / Z-score |\\n| How are two numerical features related? | Correlation / Scatter plot |\\n| How do groups differ? | Group comparison / Average by category |\\n\\n*Sources: \\\"EDA (1).pdf\\\" and \\\"EDA_Techniques_Tables.pdf\\\"*\"]',0),(1002,109,'user','What are projects mentioned in my resume','2026-11-16 08:54:42','2026-11-16 08:54:42',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(1003,109,'assistant','Based on the provided resume, the following projects are mentioned:\n\n1.  **Facebook Clone — Full Stack Social Media Application**\n    *   **Technologies:** React.js, Supabase, PostgreSQL, Supabase Auth, Supabase Realtime, Supabase Storage, RLS, Bootstrap 5.\n    *   **Features:** User authentication, profiles, posts, comments, friendships, messaging, notifications, post management (privacy, likes, saves, shares, replies, mentions), friend requests, user blocking, instant messaging, real-time notifications, and administrative moderation (reporting, suspension, restoration).\n\n2.  **Real-Time Chat Application**\n    *   **Technologies:** MERN Stack & WebRTC.\n\n3.  **Task and To-Do Dashboard (inferred from description)**\n    *   **Technologies:** Multer (for profile image uploads).\n    *   **Features:** Interactive dashboard displaying task and to-do statistics (completed/pending items).\n\n4.  **ResumEase — AI-Powered HR Assistant**\n    *   **Technologies:** Python, Django, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5, JavaScript, SQLite, AI/ML.\n    *   **Features:** Resume screening, interview scheduling, resume uploading, candidate information extraction, skill-matching, candidate shortlisting (based on education, skills, and experience), and responsive dashboards for recruiters and users.\n\n*Source: resume.pdf*','2026-11-16 08:54:48','2026-11-16 08:54:48',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"Based on the provided resume, the following projects are mentioned:\\n\\n1.  **Facebook Clone — Full Stack Social Media Application**\\n    *   **Technologies:** React.js, Supabase, PostgreSQL, Supabase Auth, Supabase Realtime, Supabase Storage, RLS, Bootstrap 5.\\n    *   **Features:** User authentication, profiles, posts, comments, friendships, messaging, notifications, post management (privacy, likes, saves, shares, replies, mentions), friend requests, user blocking, instant messaging, real-time notifications, and administrative moderation (reporting, suspension, restoration).\\n\\n2.  **Real-Time Chat Application**\\n    *   **Technologies:** MERN Stack & WebRTC.\\n\\n3.  **Task and To-Do Dashboard (inferred from description)**\\n    *   **Technologies:** Multer (for profile image uploads).\\n    *   **Features:** Interactive dashboard displaying task and to-do statistics (completed/pending items).\\n\\n4.  **ResumEase — AI-Powered HR Assistant**\\n    *   **Technologies:** Python, Django, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5, JavaScript, SQLite, AI/ML.\\n    *   **Features:** Resume screening, interview scheduling, resume uploading, candidate information extraction, skill-matching, candidate shortlisting (based on education, skills, and experience), and responsive dashboards for recruiters and users.\\n\\n*Source: resume.pdf*\"]',0),(1004,109,'user','What is MCP','2026-11-16 08:54:58','2026-11-16 08:54:58',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(1005,109,'assistant','**MCP** stands for **Model Context Protocol**.\n\nIt is an open standard introduced by Anthropic in late 2024. Its primary purpose is to simplify and standardize how AI models (like Claude) connect to external data sources, tools, and applications.\n\n### Why was MCP created?\nBefore MCP, if you wanted to connect an AI agent to a specific database, a project management tool (like Jira), or a cloud storage service (like Google Drive), developers had to build custom integrations for every single connection. This was repetitive, time-consuming, and difficult to maintain.\n\nMCP solves this by creating a **universal \"plug-and-play\" language** for AI agents.\n\n### How it works\nThink of MCP like a **USB port for AI**:\n*   **For Developers:** Instead of writing a unique connector for every AI model, you build one \"MCP server\" for your data or tool. Once it\'s built, any AI application that supports the MCP standard can instantly \"plug in\" and use that data or tool.\n*   **For AI Agents:** It gives the agent a standardized way to request information, search files, or execute actions across different systems without needing custom code for each one.\n\n### Key Benefits\n*   **Interoperability:** It allows different AI tools and data sources to work together seamlessly.\n*   **Efficiency:** Developers only need to build an integration once.\n*   **Contextual Awareness:** By easily connecting to internal company databases or personal files, AI agents become much more \"context-aware,\" meaning they can provide answers based on your specific information rather than just general knowledge.\n\nIn short, **MCP is the infrastructure that makes AI agents more useful** by making it incredibly easy for them to access the tools and data they need to perform real work.\n\n### Sources\n[1] [Model Context Protocol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_Context_Protocol)\n[2] [Agent harness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent_harness)\n[3] [LiteLLM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LiteLLM)\n[4] [Agent2Agent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent2Agent)\n[5] [OpenAI Codex](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenAI_Codex)\n[6] [Agent-based commerce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent-based_commerce)\n[7] [NLWeb](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NLWeb)\n[8] [Anthropic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthropic)\n[9] [Claude (AI)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_(AI))\n[10] [AI agent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_agent)','2026-11-16 08:55:04','2026-11-16 08:55:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"The **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** is an open standard designed to simplify how AI models connect to data and tools. It was introduced by Anthropic in November 2024.\\n\\n### Why do we need it?\\nBefore MCP, if a developer wanted to connect an AI application to various data sources (like databases, internal company documents, or third-party APIs), they had to build custom integrations for every single source. This was inefficient, time-consuming, and difficult to maintain.\\n\\nMCP solves this by providing a universal, standardized language for these connections.\\n\\n### How it works\\nThink of MCP like a **USB-C port for AI applications**. Just as a USB-C cable lets you plug almost any device into a computer, MCP lets an AI agent \\\"plug in\\\" to any data source or tool that supports the protocol.\\n\\n*   **MCP Servers:** These act as the \\\"connectors\\\" for specific data or tools (e.g., a Google Drive server, a Postgres database server, or a Slack integration server).\\n*   **MCP Clients:** These are the AI applications or agents (like Claude Desktop or other AI tools) that want to access the information from those servers.\\n\\n### Key Benefits\\n*   **Universal Compatibility:** Once a developer builds an MCP server for a tool, that tool can be used by any AI application that supports the MCP standard.\\n*   **Easier Development:** Developers don\'t need to write custom integration code for every new AI model or tool they want to use.\\n*   **Data Access:** It allows AI models to securely access and interact with real-time data, internal systems, and external tools without the need for complex, proprietary integrations.\\n\\nIn short, MCP makes it much easier for AI agents to gain \\\"contextual awareness\\\"—the ability to pull the right information from the right place at the right time—which is essential for building more capable and autonomous AI systems.\\n\\n### Sources\\n[1] [Model Context Protocol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_Context_Protocol)\\n[2] [Agent harness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent_harness)\\n[3] [LiteLLM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LiteLLM)\\n[4] [Agent2Agent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent2Agent)\\n[5] [Agent-based commerce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent-based_commerce)\\n[6] [NLWeb](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NLWeb)\\n[7] [OpenAI Codex](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenAI_Codex)\\n[8] [Anthropic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthropic)\\n[9] [Claude (AI)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_(AI))\\n[10] [AI agent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_agent)\", \"**MCP** stands for **Model Context Protocol**.\\n\\nIt is an open standard introduced by Anthropic in late 2024. Its primary purpose is to simplify and standardize how AI models (like Claude) connect to external data sources, tools, and applications.\\n\\n### Why was MCP created?\\nBefore MCP, if you wanted to connect an AI agent to a specific database, a project management tool (like Jira), or a cloud storage service (like Google Drive), developers had to build custom integrations for every single connection. This was repetitive, time-consuming, and difficult to maintain.\\n\\nMCP solves this by creating a **universal \\\"plug-and-play\\\" language** for AI agents.\\n\\n### How it works\\nThink of MCP like a **USB port for AI**:\\n*   **For Developers:** Instead of writing a unique connector for every AI model, you build one \\\"MCP server\\\" for your data or tool. Once it\'s built, any AI application that supports the MCP standard can instantly \\\"plug in\\\" and use that data or tool.\\n*   **For AI Agents:** It gives the agent a standardized way to request information, search files, or execute actions across different systems without needing custom code for each one.\\n\\n### Key Benefits\\n*   **Interoperability:** It allows different AI tools and data sources to work together seamlessly.\\n*   **Efficiency:** Developers only need to build an integration once.\\n*   **Contextual Awareness:** By easily connecting to internal company databases or personal files, AI agents become much more \\\"context-aware,\\\" meaning they can provide answers based on your specific information rather than just general knowledge.\\n\\nIn short, **MCP is the infrastructure that makes AI agents more useful** by making it incredibly easy for them to access the tools and data they need to perform real work.\\n\\n### Sources\\n[1] [Model Context Protocol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_Context_Protocol)\\n[2] [Agent harness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent_harness)\\n[3] [LiteLLM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LiteLLM)\\n[4] [Agent2Agent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent2Agent)\\n[5] [OpenAI Codex](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenAI_Codex)\\n[6] [Agent-based commerce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent-based_commerce)\\n[7] [NLWeb](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NLWeb)\\n[8] [Anthropic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthropic)\\n[9] [Claude (AI)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_(AI))\\n[10] [AI agent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_agent)\"]',1),(1006,109,'user','What is supabase, compare EDA and Supabase','2026-11-16 08:55:41','2026-11-16 08:55:41',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(1007,109,'assistant','### What is Supabase?\nBased on the provided resume, **Supabase** is a backend-as-a-service platform used to build full-stack applications. It provides a suite of tools that replace the need to build a custom backend from scratch. Key components mentioned in your resume include:\n\n*   **Database:** Uses PostgreSQL, a powerful relational database.\n*   **Authentication:** Supabase Auth handles user login and security.\n*   **Realtime:** Supabase Realtime allows for instant data synchronization, such as live messaging and notifications.\n*   **Storage:** Supabase Storage is used for managing files and media.\n*   **Security:** It supports **Row Level Security (RLS)**, which allows developers to control access to specific data rows, ensuring users only see or modify the data they are authorized to access.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison: EDA vs. Supabase\n\nIt is important to note that **EDA and Supabase serve completely different purposes** in the development lifecycle. One is a data analysis process, while the other is a platform for building applications.\n\n| Feature | Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) | Supabase |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **What is it?** | A data analysis process/methodology. | A backend-as-a-service (BaaS) platform. |\n| **Primary Goal** | To understand, clean, and find patterns in data. | To host databases, manage users, and store data for apps. |\n| **When is it used?** | Before building an ML model or performing deeper analysis. | During the development and production of an application. |\n| **Key Techniques/Tools** | Univariate/Bivariate analysis, histograms, correlation, mean/median. | PostgreSQL, Auth, Realtime, Storage, RLS. |\n| **Output** | Insights, data quality reports, and visualizations. | A functional, secure, and data-driven application backend. |\n\n#### How they might relate in a project:\nWhile they are different, they can be part of the same workflow. For example:\n1.  You build a social media application (like your **Facebook Clone**) using **Supabase** to store user posts and comments.\n2.  After collecting a large amount of data in your Supabase database, you might export that data to perform **EDA** on it.\n3.  The **EDA** would help you understand user behavior (e.g., \"What is the average number of posts per user?\") to help you optimize the application or build a recommendation system.\n\n*Sources: \"resume.pdf\", \"EDA (1).pdf\", and \"EDA_Techniques_Tables.pdf\"*','2026-11-16 08:55:48','2026-11-16 08:56:20',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"**Supabase (as described in your resume)**  \\n- Mentioned in the *Facebook Clone* project.  \\n- Listed among the technologies: **React.js, Supabase, PostgreSQL, Supabase Auth, Supabase Realtime, Supabase Storage, RLS, Bootstrap 5**.  \\n- Used for:\\n  * User authentication (Supabase Auth)  \\n  * Real‑time features such as instant messaging and notifications (Supabase Realtime)  \\n  * File handling/storage (Supabase Storage)  \\n  * Securing data with **Row‑Level Security (RLS)** and protected routes.  \\n*Source: resume.pdf*  \\n\\n**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) (from the EDA reference documents)**  \\n- Defined as the **process of examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset** before further analysis or model building.  \\n- **Purpose**: discover patterns, missing data, outliers, relationships, data quality, distribution, and decide next steps.  \\n- **Techniques** (selected examples):\\n  * *Univariate* – analyze one variable at a time (mean, median, histogram, box plot, etc.).  \\n  * *Bivariate* – analyze two variables to understand relationships (correlation, scatter plot, cross‑tabulation, etc.).  \\n  * *Multivariate* – (not detailed in the excerpts, but implied as analysis of three or more variables).  \\n- **Applications** include market analysis, risk assessment, quality control, healthcare analytics, recommendation systems, etc.  \\n*Sources: EDA_Techniques_Tables.pdf; EDA (1).pdf*  \\n\\n---\\n\\n### Comparison: Supabase vs. EDA  \\n\\n| Aspect | Supabase (as per your resume) | Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) |\\n|--------|------------------------------|---------------------------------|\\n| **Domain** | Backend‑as‑a‑service platform for building web/app features (auth, realtime, storage). | Data‑analysis methodology for understanding datasets. |\\n| **Primary Goal** | Provide ready‑made services (authentication, realtime messaging, file storage, security) to accelerate app development. | Gain insight into data quality, distribution, and relationships before modeling or decision‑making. |\\n| **Key Components** | • Supabase Auth (user login)  <br>• Supabase Realtime (live updates) <br>• Supabase Storage (file handling) <br>• Row‑Level Security (RLS) with PostgreSQL | • Statistical summaries (mean, median, etc.) <br>• Visualizations (histograms, box plots, scatter plots) <br>• Techniques for univariate, bivariate, multivariate analysis |\\n| **Typical Use Cases** | Building a social‑media app, real‑time chat, any web app needing auth/storage without writing backend from scratch. | Analyzing a dataset (e.g., Titanic data) to detect outliers, understand distributions, or prepare data for machine‑learning models. |\\n| **Output** | Functional application features (e.g., user accounts, live chat). | Insights, visual reports, and data‑cleaning decisions. |\\n| **Relationship** | Supabase can **store** the data that later might be subjected to EDA, but they serve different stages of a project (implementation vs. analysis). | EDA does not provide infrastructure; it works on whatever data source is available (which could be a Supabase database). |\\n\\n**Bottom line:**  \\n- **Supabase** is a set of backend services that help you quickly add authentication, real‑time communication, and storage to an application.  \\n- **EDA** is a systematic approach to explore and understand a dataset before any modeling or business decision.  \\n\\nThey complement each other (Supabase can host the data you later explore with EDA) but address distinct problems.\", \"### What is Supabase?\\nBased on the provided resume, **Supabase** is a backend-as-a-service platform used to build full-stack applications. It provides a suite of tools that replace the need to build a custom backend from scratch. Key components mentioned in your resume include:\\n\\n*   **Database:** Uses PostgreSQL, a powerful relational database.\\n*   **Authentication:** Supabase Auth handles user login and security.\\n*   **Realtime:** Supabase Realtime allows for instant data synchronization, such as live messaging and notifications.\\n*   **Storage:** Supabase Storage is used for managing files and media.\\n*   **Security:** It supports **Row Level Security (RLS)**, which allows developers to control access to specific data rows, ensuring users only see or modify the data they are authorized to access.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Comparison: EDA vs. Supabase\\n\\nIt is important to note that **EDA and Supabase serve completely different purposes** in the development lifecycle. One is a data analysis process, while the other is a platform for building applications.\\n\\n| Feature | Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) | Supabase |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **What is it?** | A data analysis process/methodology. | A backend-as-a-service (BaaS) platform. |\\n| **Primary Goal** | To understand, clean, and find patterns in data. | To host databases, manage users, and store data for apps. |\\n| **When is it used?** | Before building an ML model or performing deeper analysis. | During the development and production of an application. |\\n| **Key Techniques/Tools** | Univariate/Bivariate analysis, histograms, correlation, mean/median. | PostgreSQL, Auth, Realtime, Storage, RLS. |\\n| **Output** | Insights, data quality reports, and visualizations. | A functional, secure, and data-driven application backend. |\\n\\n#### How they might relate in a project:\\nWhile they are different, they can be part of the same workflow. For example:\\n1.  You build a social media application (like your **Facebook Clone**) using **Supabase** to store user posts and comments.\\n2.  After collecting a large amount of data in your Supabase database, you might export that data to perform **EDA** on it.\\n3.  The **EDA** would help you understand user behavior (e.g., \\\"What is the average number of posts per user?\\\") to help you optimize the application or build a recommendation system.\\n\\n*Sources: \\\"resume.pdf\\\", \\\"EDA (1).pdf\\\", and \\\"EDA_Techniques_Tables.pdf\\\"*\"]',1),(1008,108,'user','What is EDA?','2026-11-16 09:01:40','2026-11-16 09:01:40',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[]',0),(1009,108,'assistant','[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** is the process of **examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset** before it is used for further analysis or to build a machine‑learning model.\\n\\nThe document **“EDA (1).pdf**” defines EDA as:\\n* Exploring the data to understand its contents, discover patterns, identify missing values, outliers, and relationships, and assess data quality and distribution.\\n* Its purpose is to answer questions such as “What data do I have?”, “Is my data correct and complete?”, “How is my data distributed?”, and “What should I do next?”.\\n\\nIn short, EDA is the initial, systematic step that helps you get to know your data and prepare it for any downstream analysis or modeling.\"}]','2026-11-16 09:01:45','2026-11-16 09:01:45',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[]','[\"[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** is the process of **examining, understanding, cleaning, and visualizing a dataset** before it is used for further analysis or to build a machine‑learning model.\\\\n\\\\nThe document **“EDA (1).pdf**” defines EDA as:\\\\n* Exploring the data to understand its contents, discover patterns, identify missing values, outliers, and relationships, and assess data quality and distribution.\\\\n* Its purpose is to answer questions such as “What data do I have?”, “Is my data correct and complete?”, “How is my data distributed?”, and “What should I do next?”.\\\\n\\\\nIn short, EDA is the initial, systematic step that helps you get to know your data and prepare it for any downstream analysis or modeling.\\\"}]\"]',0);
